window.FLOCK_DOC_SEARCH = [{"title":"Start here","url":"index.html","text":"FLOCK documentation This is the starting point for humans and agents working on the robotic-herding game. Updated 14 October 2026. [Open the HTML documentation](html/index.html) for linked pages, search, diagrams, code examples and a page table of contents. Open that file directly after cloning; no application server is required. Read in this order Document Use it for ------ [Requirements](REQUIREMENTS.md) Product intent, implemented behavior, pending requirements and open decisions [Architecture](ARCHITECTURE.md) Module ownership, shared contracts and safe change boundaries [Gameplay](systems/GAMEPLAY.md) Input, simulation, companions, rewards, profile and upgrades [World](systems/WORLD.md) Procedural routes, barriers, footprints and navigation [Presentation](systems/PRESENTATION.md) Rendering, animation, nature, weather and audio [Development and release](DEVELOPMENT.md) Build, tests, documentation generation, QA evidence and publishing [Issue tracker](ISSUES.md) Prioritized work, acceptance criteria and agent handoffs [Quality record](QUALITY.md) Historical reviews and the boundaries of their evidence [Documentation reviews](REVIEW.md) Blind content/HTML findings, corrections and verification limits Source of truth Runtime behavior comes from source and tests. The requirements describe intended behavior and identify gaps; they do not make requested features exist. The issue tracker records pending work. Historical quality scores apply only to the reviewed revision. HTML is generated from Markdown; edit the Markdown and run npm run docs:build. F-025 is the highest-priority pending implementation: the required public deployment URL is https://novydhillon.github.io/Flock/dev2/. The current game is still at the old nested path, https://novydhillon.github.io/Flock/flock/dev2/. This documentation update does not migrate the game. Examples and diagrams Node examples are intended to run from the repository root with dependencies installed. Snippets labeled illustrative describe a contract rather than a supported public API. Diagram arrows show ownership, calls or transitions, not network services. This application has no backend. The HTML copy uses local assets and a bundled Mermaid renderer. Without JavaScript, documentation text and code remain readable; diagram source remains available. Source links in the HTML point back to files in the cloned repository. The repository HTML files are viewable locally; GitHub's file browser displays their source rather than serving them as a website."},{"title":"Requirements","url":"requirements.html","text":"Flock product requirements Snapshot: 14 October 2026; source d941bf2 on flock_3D_chatgpt-work. Audience: implementers, reviewers and players checking intended behavior. Public campaign levels are 1–100; source indexes are 0–99. This document separates the implemented baseline from requested changes. Implemented baseline means present in the inspected source, not proven on a phone. Pending request means recorded user intent whose acceptance criteria are still open. Proposed / unresolved means an implementation choice that must not be presented as approved. All F-001–F-025 remain Open in [ISSUES.md](ISSUES.md), which owns issue priority, detailed acceptance criteria and execution records. [QUALITY.md](QUALITY.md) owns historical test/review evidence and its limits. This document does not implement or deploy anything. Product and approved engine Flock: Little Robot, Big Pastures is a cheerful, true-3D robotic herding game for portrait phones, playable with one thumb. The player steers Pip, a robotic sheepdog, behind a flock to guide it along countryside trails to a pen, fenced field or barn. Rewards buy skills and companions; companions support the player rather than delivering the flock unattended. Preserve the rounded, colorful, handmade countryside and visible robotic character details. The requested visual direction was bright, organic, clay/stop-motion countryside inspired by Aardman; characters and assets remain original. The original engine request was ThreeRust. Investigation found no publicly documented reusable runtime SDK suitable for this repository; the user approved Three.js as the fallback. The implemented stack is Three.js/WebGL, JavaScript modules and Vite, with procedural geometry/textures and generated audio. Do not describe this as ThreeRust, three-rs or threers. See [README.md](../README.md) and [package.json](../package.json). The current product is a standalone static development build with local browser saves. Accounts, online multiplayer and cloud saves are outside the implemented baseline. A development build does not currently imply the requested QA tools exist. Implemented baseline to preserve Play and state - Drag the meadow or movement control to steer; WASD/arrows support desktop. Tap Woof, double-tap the meadow or press Space to bark. Bark applies a short herding burst; the current controller uses a 650 ms burst and 2,100 ms cooldown. Preserve single-thumb access when adding interactions. - Pip's proximity pushes animals away; grouping behavior respects barriers. Physical bodies cannot cross fences, bridge rails, rivers outside decks, trees or the map perimeter. Pip's signal may cross low wooden fences/rails, but barns, water and trees shield it. Companion attendance, movement and delivery use clear ground paths. - Delivery currently requires Pip near the destination, an unheld animal inside a radial goal and clear ground attendance. Saved animals stop moving. The level currently ends only when every animal is saved. There is elapsed-time display, no arrival countdown or partial-delivery score; F-009 and F-010 intentionally change that contract. - Pause/menu/workshop suspend gameplay and audio. Workshop and results support replay/continuation. Restart meadow resets the current scenario; confirmed Restart Game clears campaign progress, bolts and purchases and starts level 1. Cancelling preserves progress. - Music begins after user interaction and shares a mute control with wildlife/weather ambience. Pending audio activation must not undo pause; delayed music scheduling must stay bounded. Code: [main.js](../src/main.js) (begin, bark, menu, confirmRestart, complete, frame), [simulation.js](../src/simulation.js) (stepSimulation), [audio.js](../src/audio.js). Regressions: UI, simulation, audio and herding-journey tests under [tests/](../tests/). This diagram summarizes current player states. Workshop return follows its originating gameplay state: opening it through a paused gameplay menu and closing it resumes play; an intro visit returns to intro, and results return to results. The controller also opens the workshop directly from play. Restart confirmation/cancellation is omitted here. Timeout completion is pending F-009. Campaign, map size and routes The recorded user baseline asks for a small sheep-to-pen tutorial, increasing flock size and journey distance, procedural fields/rivers/bridges/roads/gates, and progression points unlocking skills and autonomous helpers. Later direction preserves compact single-path levels 1–5, asks for 1–2 routes at levels 6–15 and 1–3 routes from level 16, expands vertical roaming bounds by 3–4× from level 6, and continues increasing the playable expanse. Drone starts at 10; the more expensive Buddy starts at 20; other skills appear across the first fifteen levels. These are intent constraints. The table below records current generator rules, not newly proposed balance targets. Exact starting count 5, +3 animals per level and cap 65 are implementation-derived, not additional user-specified numbers. The current late generator chooses two or three routes within the requested one-to-three range; it does not generate single-route late levels. Deterministic default seed is index 7919 + 42; custom seeds are supported by createLevel. Index is clamped to 0–99. Aspect Current implementation ------ Animal count min(5 + 3 index, 65): levels 1–5 have 5, 8, 11, 14, 17; level 21 onward has 65. Journey distance continues growing. Species Sheep initially; from level 4 every fourth animal (ID divisible by 4) is a cow. Compact levels 1–5 Canonical bounds: travel axis −32 to 30 + 3 index, cross axis −24 to +24. At level 1 this is 62 × 48 world units; at level 5, 74 × 48. Expanded levels 6–100 Canonical travel bounds −40 to 63 + 3 index; half cross extent 84 + 14 floor(index / 15). Level 6 is 118 × 168 before rotation/final bounds adjustment. The generator additionally expands final Z bounds to cover ±half cross extent. Destination distance Canonical destination axis 15 + 3 index for levels 1–5, 48 + 3 index thereafter; spawn is −20 early, −26 later. These are world-coordinate distances, not completion times. Route count Levels 1–5: one. Levels 6–15: one or two (zero-based odd indexes get two). Level 16 onward: two or three (indexes divisible by 3 get three). Direction First level fixed; other early levels use two opposing orientations; later levels rotate across four compass directions according to seed. Water/gates Level 1 has no river; other early levels one. Later levels one until level 15, two from level 16. Gate openings begin at level 3; operable closed gates are pending F-006. Destinations Repeat pen, field, barn by index modulo 3. Current target radius is 4.7 + 0.2 sqrt(animal count); target fences are square with an entrance. F-010 replaces the mismatched radial goal. Roads and bridges Roads are 4.1 units wide early, 4.8 later. Current bridge widths: level 2 = 11.5; levels 3–5 = 8.5; later = 10. Length is river width + 5. F-005 supersedes these crossing dimensions/alignment. Examples: level 8 is index 7, has 26 robots and two routes; level 17 is index 16 and is the first level eligible for the requested, currently absent bolting behavior. The special wide level-2 crossing repaired a demonstrated simulated retrieval stall; preserve that regression when changing bridge rules. Maps provide alternate trails, winding roads, real bridge/gate openings, destination staging room, continuous rivers beyond boundaries and decorative hills outside playable ground. Alternate routes are generated by level rules, not purchased or independently unlocked. See [world.js](../src/world.js) (createLevel, canOccupy, terrainHeight, navigationTarget, openingSteer) and world/journey tests. Geometry-route reachability is not proof that every human can deliver every flock. Progress, economy and helpers The initial profile has zero bolts, no upgrades, no completed levels and only level 1 available. Finishing a meadow unlocks the next one (capped at level 100). The current first-completion reward is 80 + 25 index bolts; replay gives no duplicate reward. Purchased tiers remain saved, but only tiers unlocked for the meadow being played apply. Invalid profile fields are sanitized; legacy boolean purchases migrate to tier 1. Browser storage failure retains the session and shows feedback. Key: flock-foundry-v1; do not clear or rename it for F-025. Skill Tier unlock levels Tier costs (bolts) Current tier effects ------------ Turbo paws 2 / 6 / 11 80 / 120 / 200 +1.8 / +2.7 / +3.5 m/s Friendly signal 7 / 10 / 15 120 / 180 / 260 +2 / +3 / +4 m range Scout drone 10 / 14 / 18 200 / 360 / 540 Support strength 0.7 / 0.93 / 1.17 Buddy dog 20 / 24 / 28 600 / 850 / 1200 Support strength 1 / 1.3 / 1.58 Happy bark 4 / 8 / 13 100 / 160 / 240 +0.25 / +0.45 / +0.65 strength Flock harmony 12 / 14 / 15 160 / 220 / 320 +12% / +23% / +35% regrouping Drone unlock is exactly level 10; Buddy is exactly 20 and costs more. Helpers attend the largest connected herd, hold it when Pip leaves, and do not fetch isolated stragglers or autonomously finish. Recover stragglers and return to lead delivery. Ground Buddy must navigate around solid barriers. See [progression.js](../src/progression.js), profile functions in [simulation.js](../src/simulation.js), progression/companion tests. Art, wildlife and weather Current sheep/cows have articulated, instanced stop-motion leg poses. Ground uses deterministic tileable color/bump textures, with decorative grass, flowers and reeds. Puffy model clouds drift with soft ground shadows. Butterflies, birds and simple squirrels avoid nearby robots, including saved flock animals and helpers. There are no rabbits, requested bushes or weather-driven shelter system yet. weatherForLevel in [nature.js](../src/nature.js) uses sun for levels 1–5, then cycles sun/wind/rain/sun/snow, disallowing snow through level 8. Non-sun intensity is currently 0.3; weather is fixed per level and does not affect movement or rewards. Current counts are five clouds, five birds, three squirrels, and eight butterflies (four in rain, zero in snow), subject to safe placement. These fixed counts do not satisfy F-017's random area-scaled populations. Animated rainfall is not accumulated wetness, storms or puddles. Requested change traceability Every row below is Pending / Open. Existing related systems are described to prevent confusing partial support with completed acceptance. Detailed reproduction evidence, dependencies and decisions are in the corresponding sections of [ISSUES.md](ISSUES.md). ID Priority Required outcome and exact constraints Current gap / main code touchpoints ------------ F-025 P0, first Canonical public game at https://novydhillon.github.io/Flock/dev2/; deploy dev2/ on gh-pages, preserve unrelated files and saved profiles; explicitly migrate old URL. Not implemented. Script still targets flock/dev2/; README describes old path. scripts/deploy-pages.mjs, build/deployment configuration, links. F-001 P1 Continuous textured path corners without black triangles, gaps, spikes or flicker; check reported levels 4/8. Screenshots show artifacts; temporal flicker/root cause still require rendering. render.js ribbon geometry. F-002 P1 Bark above grass/path/ground and bridge decks, normally occluded by fences/rails. Ring currently fixed at world height .08. render.js bark/surface height; preserve depth testing. F-003 P2 Detailed recognizable squirrel; coordinated scurry/bound/alert motion, stable idle/blocked heading, no spinning. Simple art and desired-direction heading exist. nature.js, models.js; actual motion review required. F-004 P2 Subtle animated running-water texture following local river direction, seamless banks/bends, pause-safe. Rivers exist; requested flow treatment pending. render.js, world.js. F-005 P1 Crossings perpendicular to local river tangent; bridge width 1.25 × crossing path width; minimal safe dry-ground extension. Current crossings use fixed canonical heading/dimensions. Share visual/collision footprint; retain level-2 retrieval. world.js, models.js, render.js. F-006 P2 Gates requiring player opening at level 20+; visible/collidable state and single-thumb interaction. Levels 1–19 retain accessible openings. Existing gates are openings, no open/close state. world.js, simulation.js, main.js, renderer. F-007 P2 Stone walls replace fences on selected levels with consistent collision and usable entrances. Fence baseline exists; wall variants pending. world.js, models.js, render.js. F-008 P2 Soft textured clouds, potentially overlapping blurred blobs, 10–25% semi-transparency, drifting/shadowed and readable. Model clouds currently use .5 material opacity. Opacity interpretation needs confirmation. nature.js. F-009 P1 First valid arrival starts 30–60 s countdown by complexity; finish at zero or all delivered; partial score shown as score / possible score. Full-flock-only completion/reward currently. simulation.js, main.js, progression.js, profile/UI. F-010 P1 Highlight, delivery region, minimap and rotated pen/field/barn enclosure share the same shape. Radial highlight/admission mismatch fenced enclosure. world.js, render.js, simulation.js. F-011 P2 Pip tail light normally blue, flashes red on bark then restores reliably. Dedicated tail feedback absent. models.js, render.js, shared bark event. F-012 P2 From level 8, up to 15% eligible animals have 33% chance to wander away while not actively herded. From level 17, up to 10% chance to bolt for 1–3 s. New cohort/probability behavior absent; ordinary tiny idle motion is not this feature. simulation.js, world.js. F-013 P2 Investigate level-5 pacing with humans, retrieval/chokepoint evidence and unchanged ordinary-input regressions. Simulated level-5 time spike known; no confirmed human difficulty verdict. World/journey balance. F-014 P1 Real-phone controls, portrait readability, gestures, modals, sound and restart verified with device/revision evidence. Headless tests cannot close this gap. UI/audio/rendering. F-015 P3 Measure mobile GPU and repeated transition stability on normal, large/split herds and weather scenes. Submission/resource checks exist; phone frame times unmeasured. Set device targets before verdict. F-016 P1 Under cover, fade roof and wall nearest camera when occluding Pip/flock; restore stably; retain collision. Covered-structure cutaway absent. models.js, render.js; test rotated/partial/split occupancy. F-017 P2 Add detailed hopping rabbits; random rabbit/squirrel populations scale with usable level area. Rabbits absent, squirrels fixed count. nature.js, models.js; seeded bounded generation. F-018 P1 Dev-only QA menu: level 0–100, add/remove bolts, pause and reposition individual animals using touch/mouse. Available in deployed dev build; absent in release. No QA menu. Level mapping unresolved against 1–100 campaign. Build config, main.js, simulation.js, style. F-019 P2 Organic varied bushes without blocked routes/spawns or hidden critical play areas. Bushes absent; decide decorative versus physical. world.js, models.js, render.js. F-020 P2 Visibly larger trees while maintaining proportion, actor visibility and route clearance. Existing trees retain current dimensions. Target scale unresolved. World/model/render bounds. F-021 P2 Richer tree/bush foliage and bark detail/textures, cheerful organic appearance with mobile budget. Current simplified tree baseline; bushes pending. models.js, render.js. F-022 P2 Light rain/wind retain wildlife; worsening severity makes smaller species hide first, then larger; plausible return. Fixed per-level counts lack retreat/shelter transitions. nature.js, shared weather state. F-023 P2 Worsening rain/storms increase cloud cover/change lighting; lightning flashes paired with thunder. No storm/transition/event contract. nature.js, render.js, audio.js. F-024 P2 Sustained heavier rain gradually darkens grass/dirt; storms form puddles and animal footstep splashes; rivers respond coherently. No accumulation/puddle/splash system. nature.js, render.js, world.js. Decisions still open Resolve only the choices needed for an implementation; independent repairs can proceed. Record actual approvals in ISSUES.md. The following are not approved defaults: - F-005: whether the 25% comparison excludes decorative path shoulders; safe landing overlap dimensions. - F-006/F-007: gate action and reclosing; wall selection/height and whether walls block herding signals. - F-008/F-023: whether “10–25%” means opacity 0.10–0.25, how overlapping blobs compose, and any storm exception. - F-009: complexity-to-duration formula, score per animal/species, score versus spendable bolts, partial-result unlocks, replay/best-score/reward policy and save migration. Equal points per animal is only a proposal. For example, 8 of 10 delivered at timeout must produce a stable partial result, but its numeric score/reward cannot be specified yet. - F-011: whether Buddy shares Pip's tail convention on a real Buddy bark event. - F-012: eligible-cohort integer rounding, random opportunity interval, wander distance/speed/cooldown, definition of active herding, and whether bolt chance is conditional on wandering. A 33% chance is not permission to roll every frame. For a five-animal flock, the treatment of 15% eligibility requires an explicit rounding policy. - F-017/F-019/F-020: wildlife area/count ranges, caps, absence/spawn rules; bush collision policy; tree scale/range. - F-018: exact mapping of requested QA selector 0–100 to campaign indexes 0–99; test-save isolation, invalid-placement override and scenario reset/export. Do not silently clamp 100 or invent a 101st campaign level. - F-022–F-024: shared severity model, species thresholds/hysteresis/return delay, transition timing and within-level weather policy; lightning frequency/intensity/thunder timing; wetness/drying rates, puddle thresholds/placement, river response and which actors splash. No weather movement penalty is approved. - F-025: migration behavior at the superseded URL; no independent second maintained copy. This is a required coordination boundary, not a claim that a shared severity API has already been designed or approved. Deployment requirement and release boundary F-025 precedes all other queued work. Required canonical URL: https://novydhillon.github.io/Flock/dev2/. The current recorded live location is https://novydhillon.github.io/Flock/flock/dev2/; that is historical tracker evidence, not a fresh live check performed for this document. /Flock/ is the case-sensitive GitHub Pages repository prefix, not an output subfolder to duplicate. Current npm run build uses Vite relative base ./, emits dist/, and does not require a runtime CDN/backend. .github/workflows/deploy-dev2.yml tests/packages the working branch; native Pages publishing uses gh-pages. scripts/deploy-pages.mjs currently prepares a detached Pages tree at the wrong flock/dev2/ path and does not commit/push. It must be corrected before using it for the requested release. Preserve site root, flock/dev/ and every unrelated blob; explicitly decide how the old URL migrates. Maintain the profile key and verify progress on the same origin at the new path. Source completion, production build, Pages commit, publication and live verification are separate evidence. Acceptance requires direct/fresh reload at the canonical URL, all referenced assets resolving, deployed file hashes matching the intended build, preserved unrelated Pages content, migration behavior recorded, and publication revision/workflow evidence. Documentation alone cannot close F-025 or change the tracker’s current-live claim. Verification and handoff contract For each implemented request, record requirement ID, source commit, level/seed, expected/observed outcome, relevant commands/results and remaining limitations in ISSUES.md. Run meaningful regression checks and production build. Retain the solved companion hold/no-autonomous-delivery, level-10/20 unlock, level-2 bridge retrieval, profile/restart and audio pause/scheduling cases. npm test covers source simulation/profile/controller behavior, real Three.js geometry/matrices behind a substituted GPU interface, resource disposal and synthetic audio lifecycles. npm run build checks production packaging. Existing historical evidence reports 93 passing tests, route sweeps, bounded legitimate-input flock deliveries and fresh blind reviews at 9.1/10 technical / 8.6/10 gameplay. These are bounded historical findings from QUALITY.md, not a new assessment of this document or the pending requests. Headless checks cannot verify rendered pixels/flicker, motion quality, real Safari gestures, portrait touch ergonomics, perceived sound balance or phone GPU frame time. Screenshots establish visible artifacts; they do not establish temporal flicker/spinning or a root cause. Real-device records must identify device/browser and source/deployment revision, with scenario evidence. Preserve F-013–F-015 as open until their specific evidence exists. Do not convert historical critic scores into a verified overall game score. Suggested independent work after F-025: path/bark/goal correctness (F-001/F-002/F-010), crossing contract (F-005), then timer/gates/walls/wandering with resolved design choices. Coordinate shared files and the weather contract; do not treat QA tooling as a mandatory runtime dependency of fixes. This requirements snapshot has been checked against repository source and records only; it adds no gameplay changes or new browser/device validation."},{"title":"Architecture","url":"architecture.html","text":"Architecture and system contracts [Documentation home](README.md) · [Requirements](REQUIREMENTS.md) · [Issue tracker](ISSUES.md) The current game is a static browser application: Vite bundles JavaScript, CSS and local fonts; Three.js renders WebGL 2; WebAudio synthesizes music and ambience. Browser storage holds a validated profile. There is no server, account system, multiplayer or cloud save. Module map Source Responsibility Important boundary --------- src/main.js Browser UI state, input, game loop, profile actions, effect/audio dispatch Owns mode and pause; simulation is called only while playing src/world.js Seeded levels, routes, barrier queries, body movement and navigation Shared physical geometry; render geometry must agree src/simulation.js Herd movement, companion behavior, delivery and profile functions Mutates the level/dog; returns events, not DOM operations src/progression.js Upgrade catalog, gates, costs, validation and effects Ownership is separate from activation in the selected level src/models.js Robot/scenery model construction and shared model resources Shared material changes may affect many objects src/render.js Camera, scene, static/actor batches, map, transient effects Visual state must not decide rewards or collision src/nature.js Decorative weather, clouds and wildlife Current weather does not change gameplay rewards or control src/audio.js Music scheduling, UI/game sounds and ambience lifecycle Browser activation is asynchronous and can be invalidated Source links: [controller](../src/main.js), [world](../src/world.js), [simulation](../src/simulation.js), [progression](../src/progression.js), [renderer](../src/render.js), [models](../src/models.js), [nature](../src/nature.js), [audio](../src/audio.js). Data and coordinate contracts Public campaign levels are 1–100, while level.index and saved completion entries are 0–99. The requested QA selector 0–100 remains an explicit mapping decision (F-018). Do not equate index 100 with public level 100. The ground plane uses x and z; Three.js y is elevation. Some input APIs accept y as a compatibility alias for planar z; that does not change world coordinates. Headings are radians. Bridge longitudinal/lateral coordinates derive from the heading, not an axis-aligned box. The main loop requests animation frames and caps the per-frame simulation dt at 0.05 seconds. It is a variable-step loop, not an accumulator-based fixed-tick loop. Long frame gaps do not simulate the entire elapsed wall time. Headless fixtures commonly use fixed 1/60 increments for repeatability. js // Runnable: node --input-type=module from the repository root. import { createLevel, stepSimulation } from './src/simulation.js'; const level = createLevel(0); const dog = { ...level.dog }; const result = stepSimulation(level, dog, { x: 0, z: 0 }, 1 / 60, {}); console.log({ publicLevel: level.index + 1, saved: result.saved, total: result.total }); State ownership level contains mutable runtime animals, helper state, time and completion. dog is updated by the simulation. A profile contains points, paid upgrade tiers, completed level indexes, highest unlocked index and music preference. Creating/replaying a level replaces transient state; completing one can mutate the profile once. Full Restart Game replaces profile progress with defaults, keeps music preference, persists the reset and begins the tutorial. Rendering consumes the same level and dog, adding mesh/cache/resource state of its own. Collision belongs in world/simulation queries. Minimap, target highlight and model bounds must remain consistent with the shared world; F-010 records the current circle-versus-enclosure gap. Cross-system changes Change Coordinate before implementation ------ Bridges/gates/walls Generator, physical footprint, navigation, render model, minimap and journey checks Goal shape/countdown/scoring Admission geometry, completion event, reward ledger, UI, replay policy and save migration Changing weather One severity/time contract shared by wildlife, clouds/light, audio and accumulated wetness Model size/detail Placement clearance, culling bounds, shared materials, shadows and disposal QA animal editing Pause ownership, selection/placement override, profile isolation and release gating F-009/F-012/F-018/F-022–F-024 include unresolved design semantics. Their proposed contracts are future work, not hidden runtime features. See [issue acceptance criteria](ISSUES.md). Resource and async invariants Level replacement must dispose owned geometries/materials/nature resources without destroying shared cached robot geometry. Culling must retain visible limbs and shadows as well as bodies. Temporary bark/celebration effects must finish and release resources. Audio start/resume may complete after a pause, newer activation or disposal. Generation guards invalidate stale requests. Never follow an awaited start with an unconditional resume. Music catch-up skips overdue steps arithmetically and bounds scheduling work rather than replaying a backlog. Evidence and extension points Tests run actual simulation/profile code and construct real Three.js geometry, with browser graphics/audio surfaces substituted where needed. They do not establish rendered appearance, physical Safari interactions, sound quality or mobile FPS. [Quality record](QUALITY.md) preserves those limits and historical review scores. Use [system documentation](README.md) for narrower APIs. Start work from [the tracker](ISSUES.md), claim ownership, retain regression checks and record source versus deployment milestones separately."},{"title":"Gameplay & progression","url":"gameplay.html","text":"Gameplay and progression This describes current source behavior, not the queued design in the [issue tracker](../ISSUES.md). Geometry and navigation contracts are in [WORLD.md](WORLD.md). Entry points: [simulation.js](../../src/simulation.js), [progression.js](../../src/progression.js), and browser orchestration in [main.js](../../src/main.js). State and interfaces The simulation is renderer-independent ES modules, directly importable in Node. stepSimulation(level, dog, input = {}, dt = 1 / 60, upgrades = {}) mutates the level, animals, and supplied dog; it also assigns level.dog = dog. The browser initially copies the generated dog so callers should use that assignment rather than expect the original object identity to persist. Value Contract ------ level.index Zero-based campaign index, 0–99. UI level number is index + 1. Positions/input Ground plane {x, z} in world units; input y is accepted only as a fallback for missing z. Renderer height is separate. Heading Radians, forward vector {x: sin(heading), z: cos(heading)}. Animal {id, type: 'sheep' \\ 'cow', x, z, vx, vz, saved}; stepping adds heading, alert and optional pressure timestamp. Input {x, z, bark}; direction normalized and magnitude capped at 1, so diagonals do not run faster. Result {saved, total, completed, justCompleted, reward, events, helpers, dogCollision}. Events {type:'animalSaved', id, animalType} and one {type:'levelCompleted', index, reward} per fresh level instance. stepSimulation defaults to 1/60 second and clamps supplied dt to [0, 0.1]. This is not a fixed-step browser loop. main.js calls once per animation frame using elapsed frame time capped at 0.05 seconds, with no accumulator or catch-up. Tests often choose fixed 1/60 or 0.1 steps. level.time and browser completion elapsed advance only during play, using these capped deltas; elapsed is not full wall-clock time after a long stall. Bark lasts 650 ms with a 2100 ms cooldown measured by performance.now(), independently of simulation time. Pip moves at 7.8 + speedBoost units/second, with radius 0.62. Movement collision reports blockedX/blockedZ; see the [world clearance distinction](WORLD.mdclearance-is-not-one-query). Sheep radius/speed limit are 0.55/4.2, cows 0.78/3.3. These are kinematic bodies, not a rigid-body engine. One simulation step Each active animal computes forces from a snapshot, so earlier animals' updated positions do not directly affect later ones' force inputs. Separation acts under 2 units; cohesion/alignment use visible neighbors under 18 units. Ground visibility is cached by animal pair and 0.2-unit position cells. Cohesion starts beyond 2.6 units from neighbor center; alignment retains 45% of average neighbor velocity. Unpressured animals also receive small deterministic sinusoidal wandering. Saved animals leave the active flock. Pip's base pressure radius is 7.6 + signalBoost, plus 3.3 while barking. Within it, a clear player signal produces flee pressure; bark multiplies urgency by 1.65 + barkBoost. Animals flee Pip's position, not the destination. Under pressure, openingSteer can nudge sideways toward a nearby crossing/gate when forward travel is blocked. Velocity smoothing, speed caps, solid body movement, and limited angled escape attempts then resolve movement. Companion support and admission use ground paths; do not replace those with the more permissive player signal. Companions support the player Owned companion tiers apply only after their current meadow's unlock gates. The largest connected component is selected using links of at most 6.5 units with a clear ground path (radius 0.15). Ties prefer members from the previous selection, then lowest animal ID. Recompute on active-membership changes or after more than 0.25 simulation seconds. A singleton is not a supported herd: helpers go to standby, and isolated stragglers receive no support. Attendance uses the closest ground-visible member of the selected herd, not its centroid. Visibility search is limited to 18 units, so losing a clear path yields infinite attendance distance even if Pip is physically close. Existing mode Hold decision ------ Initial/standby Hold when nearest visible member is farther than 14. Hold Stay holding at distance 12 or greater; return to assist below 12. Assist Hold beyond 18; stay assisting at 18 or below. Hold entry captures the herd centroid and each member's position. Membership change with less than half overlap also recaptures positions. Holding suppresses inherited alignment/cohesion momentum and steers members back toward stored positions. Direct pressure of at least 0.03 overrides holding for that animal. It is a bounded positional behavior, not an immutable freeze. In assist, companions tidy lateral flanks relative to Pip→centroid. The force vectors have their mean removed, preventing net propulsion toward a destination. The drone flies over the centroid; Buddy navigates solid ground alongside Pip, or alongside the herd while holding. Buddy's body has no blanket flee signal. Neither companion chooses a goal or autonomously delivers the flock. Tests: [companions.test.js](../../tests/companions.test.js) covers selection, hysteresis, fifteen-minute hold, straggler retrieval, blocked signals, zero goal dependence and unattended admission. [herding-journey.test.js](../../tests/herding-journey.test.js) uses legitimate dog input to complete five representative crossing journeys; its bounded policy does not prove all seeds/routes solvable. Admission, rewards, and profile lifecycle Admission is currently a circle check, even for rectangular-looking enclosures. An animal is saved when all are true: Pip is within target.radius + 12 of target center; the animal is not holding; its center is within target.radius - physicalRadius 0.5; and Pip→animal has a clear ground path (radius 0.15). There is no countdown or partial-delivery payout. Completion requires a nonempty flock with every animal saved. justCompleted/result reward prevent repeating the completion event on one level instance. Persistent reward deduplication is separate: applyLevelReward(profile, level) requires level.completed and an index absent from completedLevels, then adds level.reward bolts and unlocks the next meadow. Replaying a fresh instance can complete again but cannot award the same index twice. Completing index 99 keeps highestLevel at 99; the browser's next button returns to index 0. defaultProfile() returns {version:1, points:0, upgrades:{}, completedLevels:[], highestLevel:0, music:true}. validateProfile rebuilds this schema: points become bounded whole numbers 0–1e9; upgrade entries are validated; completed indexes are deduplicated/sorted and restricted to 0–99; highest unlocked index is reconciled with completions and capped at 99; only explicit music:false disables music. It does not save level positions or an in-progress run. loadProfile(storage?, key = 'flock-foundry-v1') and saveProfile(profile, storage?, key?) use a storage object with getItem/setItem (default localStorage). Invalid JSON or inaccessible storage loads defaults; saving failure returns false. The browser retains its in-memory profile and displays a toast on save failure. The same key survives normal restarts and deployment path changes on the same origin. begin(index) regenerates a meadow, resets elapsed/input/tips/bark state, and keeps profile progress. “Restart this meadow” calls it with the current index. Confirmed “Restart Game” replaces the profile with defaults while retaining music, persists it, resets reward/tap state, and begins index 0. Cancel does not reset progress. Pause/workshop modes stop stepping; the renderer can still update visual effects in intro/completion. Upgrade contract The six three-tier catalog entries and exact costs/gates live in [progression.js](../../src/progression.js). Use its exports rather than duplicate a pricing table in callers: Export Purpose ------ upgradeLevel, validateUpgrades Accept positive integer tiers (cap at 3); migrate legacy true to tier 1; drop unknown/malformed entries. upgradeCost, nextUnlockLevel Price/gate of next tier; null for unknown or maxed upgrade. upgradeAvailability Workshop status with unknown, owned, locked, points, or null reason. purchaseUpgrade Atomically purchase one tier; failed purchase preserves points. activeUpgrades, upgradeEffects Clamp owned tiers to gates of the current one-based meadow number. Purchase eligibility follows unlocked campaign progress (highestLevel and completions). Activation follows the meadow being played. Thus an owned later tier stays in the save when replaying an early meadow but its effect is inactive there. Effects are absolute arrays by tier, not cumulative additions of each previous tier. See [progression tests](../../tests/progression.test.js) and [simulation tests](../../tests/simulation.test.js). Run from repository root with Node 22+; no DOM or WebGL is needed: sh node --input-type=module \u003c\u003c'JS' import assert from 'node:assert/strict'; import { createLevel, stepSimulation, defaultProfile, applyLevelReward, purchaseUpgrade, saveProfile, loadProfile, } from './src/simulation.js'; import { activeUpgrades } from './src/progression.js'; const level = createLevel(0); // Synthetic admission fixture, as opposed to a player input policy. level.animals.forEach(a => Object.assign(a, {x:level.target.x, z:level.target.z})); const result = stepSimulation(level, {...level.target}, {}, 1/60); assert.equal(result.justCompleted, true); const profile = defaultProfile(); assert.equal(applyLevelReward(profile, level), 80); assert.equal(applyLevelReward(profile, level), 0); assert.equal(purchaseUpgrade(profile, 'speed').ok, true); assert.deepEqual(activeUpgrades(profile.upgrades, 1), {}); assert.deepEqual(activeUpgrades(profile.upgrades, 2), {speed:1}); let json; const storage = {getItem: () => json, setItem: (_key, value) => {json=value;}}; assert.equal(saveProfile(profile, storage), true); assert.deepEqual(loadProfile(storage), profile); console.log('completion, reward, tier gates and storage round trip passed'); JS Current versus proposed The [tracker](../ISSUES.md) owns future requirements and acceptance criteria: enclosure geometry/admission, arrival countdown/scoring, opening gates, wanderers, balancing and dev tooling are queued changes, not contracts implemented by this document. Source tests establish headless behavior; they do not establish real-phone feel, graphics or audio validation. Deployment URL correction is also pending in the tracker; source documentation does not establish a new live release."},{"title":"World & navigation","url":"world.html","text":"World generation, collision, and navigation Current contracts come from [world.js](../../src/world.js); [GAMEPLAY.md](GAMEPLAY.md) explains its simulation callers. The [issue tracker](../ISSUES.md) is the authority for proposed geometry and gameplay changes. This module imports neither renderer nor browser APIs. Coordinates and generated state Positions use {x, z} on a flat ground plane; vertical height belongs to rendering. Distances are Euclidean world units. Heading is radians, with forward {x:sin(h), z:cos(h)} and lateral {x:cos(h), z:-sin(h)}. Do not assume destination progress always means increasing world X. createLevel(index = 0, seed = index 7919 + 42) floors/clamps the index to 0–99 and converts the seed to unsigned 32-bit. The default parameter expression uses the argument before index normalization; pass explicit valid indexes for ordinary campaign reproducibility. The same index/seed yields deeply equal fresh state. Randomness is a private seeded generator, not Math.random(). Field Meaning ------ index, seed, title, description, reward Campaign metadata; displayed level is index + 1; reward is 80 + index 25. bounds {minX,maxX,minZ,maxZ} world-space axis-aligned playable bounds. spawn, dog, animals Flock origin, Pip origin, initial body states. target {x,z,radius,type,heading}; type cycles pen → field → barn by index modulo 3. rivers Sampled centerline points, width, rotated bridge deck descriptors. fenceSegments Physical {a,b,kind} segments: boundary, interior, target. fences currently starts empty. gates Open gap descriptors, not animated door state. routes, roads Sampled routes {id,points,length}; roads reference route points plus width. obstacles, sceneryHills Circular tree collision footprints; decorative hills outside bounds. Simulation attaches runtime state such as time, completion, helpers and visibility caches. Do not reuse a stepped level as a pristine generation fixture. Generation starts in canonical longitudinal/lateral coordinates, then rotates by quarter turns. Early indexes 0–4 have a 48-unit lateral footprint, one route and longitudinal end 15 + 3index. Index 0 points east; other early levels choose east/west from seed parity. Later levels choose all four directions by seed % 4, use longitudinal end 48 + 3index, and lateral half-width 84 + floor(index/15)14; transformed bounds also retain at least that world-Z span. Thus rotated late bounds may be larger than simply rotating the canonical rectangle. Flock count is min(5 + 3index, 65). From index 3, every fourth animal is a cow. Bodies start in a compact grid with seeded jitter and Pip behind them. If a curved bank clips a body, the generator tries up to seven half-unit backward translations of the entire grid, preserving its shape. This finite correction is tested on selected custom seeds; it is not a formal proof for every unsigned seed. Generation pipeline and routes Index 0 has no river. Indexes 1–4 have one; indexes 5–14 have one; indexes 15+ have two. River centerlines are gentle sinusoids sampled every 2 canonical lateral units, extending beyond the fenced map. Each route gets a bridge over each river. Route counts are one for early levels; thereafter one on even indexes/two on odd indexes below 15; indexes 15+ have three when divisible by 3, otherwise two. From index 2, an interior fence has open gate gaps for each route. Routes connect spawn, bridge approaches/centers, gates and destination approaches. wander samples each leg roughly every 1.2 units; ordinary legs gain seeded meanders. Legs adjoining bridges/gates or ending at target stay straight. A candidate meander is kept only if every sampled segment has 0.85-unit ground clearance against already-built geometry; otherwise that leg uses straight interpolation. Trees are added afterward, excluded from a 7 + tree.radius corridor around route segments, within 14 of spawn, or within target.radius + 10 of destination. Maximum attempted tree count is min(10 + 2index,130), so actual counts vary. Roads are visual/guidance data, not forces. Nothing in stepSimulation attracts animals toward routes or target. The route API can guide Pip and ground companions; player pressure drives delivery. Shared bridge and enclosure geometry A bridge {x,z,width,length,heading} is a straight rotated rectangle. In its local frame a body is over the deck when longitudinal distance is at most length/2 + radius and lateral distance at most width/2 - radius. The longitudinal extension lets bodies enter from dry ground; lateral contraction preserves body clearance. Bridge length is river width plus 5. Index 1 uses width 11.5, indexes 2–4 use 8.5, and later decks use 10. These current dimensions are not a claim that decks already satisfy the queued perpendicular-crossing/path-width requirement. Each side rail runs from -length/2 - 0.1 to length/2 + 0.1 at lateral ±width/2, with beam radius 0.05 and four post footprints of radius 0.065 per side. Rails remain solid beyond the wet river edge, so a body on dry land beside a deck cannot cut sideways through its end rail. It must walk around an open end. Rail and river bounds are cached in WeakMaps keyed by geometry object; treat generated descriptors as static or replace the object when editing rather than mutating cached geometry in place. terrainHeight(level,x,z) returns a deck arch 0.18 + 0.12sin(π(along/length + 0.5)) inside the zero-radius bridge footprint, otherwise 0. It does not change collision into a 3D physics system. See [render.js](../../src/render.js) for presentation of these descriptors. Destination segments form a box of half-extent target.radius + 1.4, with a 7-unit entrance on its upstream side. The target's admission circle is a separate current gameplay test; see [admission](GAMEPLAY.mdadmission-rewards-and-profile-lifecycle). A visual enclosure does not imply an exact rectangular admission region. Clearance is not one query Export What it answers ------ canOccupy(level,x,z,radius=.55) May a circular body occupy one point? Bounds inset by radius + .18, fence thickness .18, expanded trees/water and solid bridge rails all apply. Water is traversable only within a bridge footprint. moveBody(level,body,dx,dz,radius=.55) Mutates position with steps of at most .25 units, tries combined displacement, then axis sliding; returns {blockedX,blockedZ}. It does not set velocity or heading. hasClearGroundPath(level,a,b,radius=.55) Is a straight ground segment clear? Solid fences/rails, trees, water and inset endpoint bounds apply. Water checks sample at intervals no greater than .45 units. hasClearPlayerSignal(level,a,b) Radius .15 audible pressure line: ignores low fences and bridge rails; target fence segments shield only barns. Trees, water outside decks and bounds still block. separatedByBank(level,a,b) Specialized river/rail separation query, not general obstacle clearance or a substitute for ground path validation. The player signal is used for Pip's pressure only. Flock cohesion/alignment visibility, companion selection/attendance/support and destination attendance use solid ground paths. A sheep can hear Pip over a low pen rail while neither body can cross it; that does not mean a companion may pull through it or admission may ignore it. Physical separation forces between very close animals are distinct from neighbor visibility. moveBody subdivides displacement to limit tunneling; stepSimulation additionally caps dt. Neither is a swept 3D rigid-body solver. Rivers are sampled polylines with radius-expanded checks, and trees are circular footprints, not mesh intersection tests. Navigation exports Export Behavior and limit ------ distance, closestOnSegment, segmentDistance Shared ground-plane geometry; projection result includes clamped t. routeProgress(level,position,routeId?) Nearest projection on matching sampled route segments; returns route/id, segment index, point, off-route distance, arclength progress and route length; null if none. nextNavigationPoint(level,position,routeId?) First sampled point at least 7 arclength units after projection, otherwise target. This is not exactly a point seven straight-line units away. navigationTarget(level,body,desired,radius=.62) Returns desired when clear, otherwise route projection/lookahead; near bridge rails builds a small visibility graph around up to two bridges to walk around open ends. It is a local waypoint heuristic, not global pathfinding for arbitrary user geometry. destinationApproach(level,body) Oriented waypoint around enclosure walls toward upstream entrance. Used by journey-test player policy, not an animal's automatic goal drive. openingSteer(level,body,vx,vz) Adds lateral steering toward the closest opening on a nearby pressed barrier. Only used under player pressure; alias steeringOpening is also exported. Run this from repository root with Node 22+: sh node --input-type=module \u003c\u003c'JS' import assert from 'node:assert/strict'; import { createLevel, distance, canOccupy, moveBody, hasClearGroundPath, routeProgress, nextNavigationPoint, terrainHeight, } from './src/world.js'; const level = createLevel(23, 201); assert.deepEqual(level, createLevel(23, 201)); const bridge = level.rivers[0].bridges[0]; assert.equal(canOccupy(level, bridge.x, bridge.z, .8), true); assert.ok(Math.abs(terrainHeight(level, bridge.x, bridge.z) - .3) \u003c 1e-8); const route = level.routes[0], walker = {...level.spawn}; for (const point of route.points) { assert.ok(hasClearGroundPath(level, walker, point, .8)); moveBody(level, walker, point.x-walker.x, point.z-walker.z, .8); assert.ok(distance(walker, point) \u003c .001); } assert.ok(distance(walker, level.target) \u003c .001); const info = routeProgress(level, level.spawn, route.id); assert.equal(info.routeId, route.id); assert.ok(nextNavigationPoint(level, level.spawn, route.id)); console.log('deterministic world, deck height and traversable route passed'); JS Evidence and queued changes [world.test.js](../../tests/world.test.js) checks all 100 default campaign indexes for cow-clear route traversal with actual moveBody calls, safe default spawns, destination margins, exterior hills and geometry constraints. It also covers selected custom seeds, four rotations, audible-versus-solid fences and dry-end rail navigation. [simulation.test.js](../../tests/simulation.test.js) checks actual movement against banks, bounds and destination walls. [herding-journey.test.js](../../tests/herding-journey.test.js) covers five bounded full-delivery journeys. Those scopes are narrower than all possible seeds or real-device verification. Future bridge orientation/width, interactive gates, stone walls, enclosure shape matching and environment changes remain tracked in [ISSUES.md](../ISSUES.md); this document does not implement or approve them. The tracker also records the pending deployment-path correction. No documentation change is evidence of a live deployment."},{"title":"Rendering, nature & audio","url":"presentation.html","text":"Presentation: renderer, nature and audio This describes the implementation at the documentation baseline, not a promise that the open art requests are complete. The application uses Three.js 0.180.0 and generated geometry/textures; there is no ThreeRust runtime, downloaded model pipeline or recorded soundtrack. Source behavior and headless tests are inspectable; real browser rendering, phone performance and perceived sound balance remain unverified here. See [quality scope](../QUALITY.md) and the [open issue tracker](../ISSUES.md). Boundaries and frame data flow [main.js](../../src/main.js) owns the animation loop, application mode and the FarmAudio instance. Simulation owns positions and gameplay decisions. [render.js](../../src/render.js) reads the level and separate player-dog state to build scene transforms, then renders; [nature.js](../../src/nature.js) maintains its own decorative wildlife/cloud state. Wildlife never pushes the herd or awards progress. The frame interval is capped at .05 seconds. Simulation and event sounds run only in playing. The renderer receives positive dt in playing, intro and complete; other modes pass zero. Ambience updates only in playing. Thus completion/intro can animate visual scenery without advancing gameplay; a modal freezes renderer time but the frame loop still redraws. Backgrounding during play opens the menu, which pauses audio and resets input. There is no main-loop call to FarmAudio.dispose() on page teardown. The integration has this shape (abridged from main.js; mode handling is essential): js const dt = Math.min((now - last) / 1000, .05); if (mode === 'playing') { const result = stepSimulation(level, dog, input, dt, profile.upgrades); for (const event of result.events) if (event.type === 'animalSaved') audio.play('saved'); } renderer.update(level, dog, ['playing', 'intro', 'complete'].includes(mode) ? dt : 0, mode === 'intro'); if (mode === 'playing') audio.updateAmbience(dt, { weather: level.weather renderer.nature?.weather?.type 'sun' }); Interface Consumer / responsibility --- --- new MeadowRenderer(canvas) Creates WebGL renderer, camera, shared materials, scene groups and nature root. Requires browser Canvas/WebGL globals. setLevel(level) Replaces level-owned presentation resources; builds terrain, structures, scenery and species batches. update(level, dog, dt, preview=false) Camera follow, poses, nature, particles, per-instance culling, final draw. Animal array order must match the last setLevel. resize() Reads viewport size and aspect; projection changes on the next update. bark() / celebrate(x,z) Presentation effects triggered by application actions/events. drawMap(canvas, level, dog) Separate Canvas 2D map derived from world coordinates, not a scene render. Main updates it at roughly 10 Hz. MeadowNature.setLevel/update/dispose Owns cosmetic weather/fauna state and all its per-level GPU resources. FarmAudio.start/resume/pause/setMuted/play/updateAmbience/dispose Owns Web Audio lifecycle, music timer, procedural sources and cleanup. Scene construction and materials MeadowRenderer configures antialiasing, pixel ratio capped at 1.6, sRGB output, ACES filmic tone mapping and exposure 1.05. A hemisphere light and moving directional sun light the scene. The sun has a 1024-square PCF soft shadow map and an orthographic shadow volume of ±30 units. Background and fog share a pale blue color; fog spans 95–195 world units. These are configuration values, not measured quality/performance guarantees. setLevel builds a plane larger than the bounds, decorative hills, road/river ribbons, bridge meshes, target marker, fences, structures, obstacle trees and bounded decorative details. Roads and rivers use the sampled centerlines supplied by the world. Road shoulders may wander cosmetically; river width follows the physical half-width. Fence/gate/target segments come from world collision geometry. Geometry still needs visual verification at tight bends: sharing a centerline does not establish artifact-free triangulation. [models.js](../../src/models.js) supplies Pip, flock species, helpers, vegetation and structures. Its primitive geometry cache is keyed by constructor/arguments, and its material cache persists across levels. bakeModel clones transformed component geometry, merges it by material, disposes temporary clones and returns fewer meshes. Model clones share the baked geometry/materials. createAnimal bakes the body separately from four articulated legs: each leg retains limbIndex and a hip pivot, with shared material meshes in hip coordinates. Material textures are generated locally. Models use deterministic 128-square clay/wood Canvas textures. The renderer creates deterministic 512-square grass/path color and bump maps; color textures are sRGB and repeat over the ground. Nature uses generated DataTexture alpha maps for cloud shadows and precipitation. Grass/path material roughness, water material properties and static lighting do not currently accumulate rain or change with weather severity. Batching, poses and visibility Static scenery and flock animals have separate submission paths: Path Construction Per-frame submission --- --- --- Static scenery batchScenery() groups eligible terrain pieces by geometry identity + material identity. It skips world-owned geometry and transparent materials; groups smaller than two remain meshes. Tufts/reeds are explicitly instanced. trackScenery() retains immutable source matrices and transformed bounding spheres. cullScenery() compacts visible matrices into the front of each instance buffer and updates count and upload range. Sheep/cows One logical Object3D pose per animal; separate InstancedMesh for every baked body/limb material piece of each species. Original animal indices are stored in the species batch. A conservative species radius includes limb pivot envelopes. The same compacted visibleIndices order feeds every body/leg mesh; gait uses original indices, so disappearing/reappearing animals retain their phase and pose. Pip/helpers Normal articulated scene groups; helper meshes are keyed by helper type. Pip and ground helper legs/tails animate; drone propellers rotate and it bobs above ground. The map is designed for one helper per type. Nature Cloud/body/wing/shadow InstancedMesh objects plus optional precipitation Points. Rewrites instance transforms; disables automatic frustum culling. Nature does not use the renderer's custom instance compaction. The renderer creates its own camera frustum before both scenery and animal culling. Automatic aggregate InstancedMesh frustum culling is disabled for these batches. Scenery spheres receive two extra world units; flock bounds receive three, retaining edge silhouettes and nearby sun shadows. Only the compacted prefix is uploaded. This reduces submitted instances, while CPU work still scans the stored instances/animals each frame. Only batched fence parts cast shadows in the static batching path; other eligible batched scenery does not inherit arbitrary original shadow flags. Flock locomotion uses a stop-motion pose clock, Math.floor(time 12) / 12. Root bob/roll and leg swings depend on speed. The limb matrix order is important: js // Same transform contract used by createAnimal and updateAnimalBatches. const limbWorld = animalWorld.clone() .multiply(new THREE.Matrix4().makeTranslation(...pivot)) .multiply(new THREE.Matrix4().makeRotationX(gait)); World-height samples place Pip, flock and ground helpers on bridges/terrain. Saved animals remain rendered; their heading uses a fixed index-based pose. The goal marker pulses. Celebration allocates 36 short-lived confetti meshes, updated and disposed in the main renderer update. Bark uses a separate 600 ms requestAnimationFrame closure, so its animation is not governed by the renderer's paused dt. Camera and map The camera is orthographic and tilted from (center.x,36,center.z+29) toward (center.x,0,center.z-2). Normal view height is 39 in portrait aspect below .8, otherwise 36; preview height is 50 or 44. Preview follows the spawn. Gameplay follows a weighted dog/unsaved-flock center (or goal when all are saved), with leading space, smoothing, and clamps that retain Pip when a helper moves the flock far away. The sun follows the same center. These clamps are covered by projected-position tests, not physical-device readability checks. The map draws roads, continuous rivers/bridges, world fence segments, goal, every flock animal, Pip and helpers. It scales x and z independently into the map rectangle, so it is a navigation overview rather than a camera projection or a guaranteed equal-scale plan. Resource ownership and level changes Before replacement, setLevel traverses terrain and actors, disposes InstancedMesh instance resources, and disposes geometries/materials explicitly tagged ownedGeometry/ownedMaterial. Label textures are disposed via ownedLabel. It clears groups and batch/helper/particle state, then builds the next world. Cached model geometry/materials and shared fence geometry deliberately survive. Distinct resources are deduplicated with sets before disposal. Nature has an independent resources set. clear() disposes instance resources, removes children, disposes owned geometry/materials/textures, and resets state; setLevel() calls it first. dispose() additionally removes the nature root from the scene. The renderer has no comprehensive public dispose() method for its shared renderer/material/model resources. Bark's separately scheduled closure is also outside the level-owned tagging path; future lifecycle changes must account for it explicitly. Nature: implemented behavior and requested work weatherForLevel(level) accepts a string or {type} override, normalizes unknown types to sun, and disallows snow at indexes 0–7. Default public levels 1–5 are sunny; from level 6 the five-step cycle is sun, wind, rain, sun, snow. Its intensity is fixed at 0 for sun and .3 otherwise. It does not transition weather during a level or consume a requested intensity field. Current implementation Open requested behavior --- --- Five clouds, four sphere blobs each, Lambert material opacity .5; matching soft textured plane shadows drift with wind. F-008 requests soft textured clouds at 10–25% opacity. F-023 requests changing cloud coverage/lighting and bounded lightning/thunder events. Up to eight butterflies (four in rain, zero in snow), five birds and three squirrels. Fixed requested counts, with unsafe spawn slots hidden. F-017 requests recognizable hopping rabbits and random populations scaled to meadow size. Rabbits are absent. Fauna are simple sphere bodies/wings; squirrel tail uses the squirrelWing batch. Idle movement follows a home-centered orbit, with heading from desired movement. F-003 requests detailed squirrels with readable scurry/bound, alert, blocked and turning motion. Current implementation does not establish visual acceptance. Rain has 128 particles; snow has 72. Points follow the dog, fall, and wrap in a bounded local volume. Wind changes drift speed. F-022 requests severity-dependent species retreat/return. Current counts vary by weather type only; there is no shelter/severity state machine. Weather affects decorative motion/particles and audio selection. F-024 requests accumulated wetness, puddles/splashes and rain-responsive rivers. No such surface state exists. Barn has narrow rear roof pieces and solid target wall segments. F-016 requests camera-facing roof/wall cutaway while actors are occluded; there is no dynamic cutaway implementation. Spawn search is deterministic from the level seed, tries at most 180 candidates per animal, prefers locations beside routes, and requires traversable ground, distance over 5.5 from route centerlines and over 9 from initial threats. Runtime threats include Pip, all flock animals (including saved ones), and helpers. Birds flee within 10 units, other fauna within eight; squirrels use canOccupy and axis fallback, while flying species may cross water/fences. Fauna stay within a two-unit map margin. MeadowNature.update clamps its own interval to .1 seconds; explicit pause returns before any motion or transform updates. The nature layer creates eight instanced batches (cloud, shadow and body/wing for each of three species), plus optional precipitation. Zero-count batches can exist, including butterflies during snow. Tests bound its drawable objects to at most 11; that is a structural limit, not a measured GPU draw/frame-time result. Synthesized audio lifecycle [audio.js](../../src/audio.js) has no fetched samples. start() is intended for a user gesture; it lazily creates one AudioContext and three gain buses (music .42, effects .6, ambience .38) into a master gain (.65, or zero when muted). It awaits context.resume() and returns a success boolean; unsupported/restricted audio fails silently so gameplay can continue. Each activation increments activationGeneration. After asynchronous resume, activation succeeds only if its generation and context remain current and the instance is not disposed. pause(), a later start/resume, or dispose() invalidates pending completions. Main also rechecks mode after awaiting audio start, preserving a modal opened while activation was pending. Mute is independent of this state diagram: it changes master gain, stops ambience immediately, and prevents play()/ambience source creation. It does not stop the music scheduler; music can continue advancing behind zero master gain. Pause stops the scheduler, stops/disconnects ambience, and fades the music bus toward .0001; it leaves the AudioContext running and permits UI effects. Already scheduled music sources are not individually canceled by pause. Resume restarts scheduling at the current sequence step with a fresh initial delay. Music runs at tempo 106 over 128 eighth-note steps: eight bars with C/F/C/G/Am/F/G/C roots and generated bass, pluck and melody voices. _note creates an oscillator/envelope, schedules its stop, and disconnects both on end; marimba adds a quieter upper partial. Effects select short note phrases or the two synthesized bark sweeps. Scheduling is bounded in two ways: there is at most one 100 ms interval, and each callback schedules at most eight steps within a .25 second lookahead. If late, it advances the modulo-128 sequence mathematically past overdue steps instead of creating old oscillators. Invalid tempos return without scheduling. Per-frame ambience creates no interval and never creates an AudioContext. Birds use two or three short sine sweeps with variation; each lasts .17 seconds. Their countdown subtracts at most .25 seconds per ambience update, so a large frame gap cannot replay a backlog. Normal gaps are 4–10 seconds; rain uses 10–20 seconds and suppresses chirps at intensity ≥.7. Bird chirps are independent of visual bird instances/positions. Weather loops one shared two-second generated noise buffer through a low-pass filter; gains scale by clamped intensity (rain .032, snow .012, wind .024 before bus/master gain). Weather changes stop/disconnect the previous chain. Main passes level.weather when present, otherwise the nature weather type, not its complete configuration. Audio defaults missing intensity to .3 and accepts/clamps explicit intensity, whereas nature currently fixes intensity by type. A future shared weather transition contract must resolve this difference rather than assuming both consumers already share severity. dispose() is idempotent and final: it invalidates activation, clears the interval, releases ambience synchronously, stops/disconnects tracked sources, closes the context and drops node/buffer references. Subsequent start() returns false. Verification and known defects These targeted checks run without a browser from the repository root: sh node --test tests/render.test.js tests/nature.test.js tests/audio.test.js [render.test.js](../../tests/render.test.js) constructs actual Three.js geometry/matrices with Canvas and WebGL substitutes. It checks finite poses/cameras, portrait Pip retention, world-resource disposal without disposing shared animal/fence geometry, bounded scenery submissions, conservative instance/shadow retention, bounded route-adjacent detail, animal compaction and articulated pose restoration. Counts are geometry/submission evidence; they do not establish FPS, correct pixels or artifact-free transparency. [nature.test.js](../../tests/nature.test.js) checks deterministic weather bounds/snow gating, safe spawning, fleeing from saved animals/helpers, squirrel occupancy, matching cloud-shadow positions, pause freezing and one-time resource disposal. [audio.test.js](../../tests/audio.test.js) uses fake contexts/parameters/timers to check competing pending activation, pause/dispose races, bounded overdue scheduling, one reusable context/timer/noise buffer, chirp limits, mute/pause effects and cleanup. Those substitutes do not verify browser autoplay behavior, audible timbre or listening balance. Reported visual defects remain open in [ISSUES.md](../ISSUES.md): path-corner black triangles/flicker (F-001), bark clipping relative to bridge/ground surfaces (F-002), and squirrel appearance/motion (F-003). The bark code explicitly assigns .08 world height after copying Pip's position; its expanding ring needs surface-aware verification. Ribbon self-overlap, degenerate triangles, z-fighting and shadow behavior are investigation candidates for F-001, not confirmed causes. Real rendered views of levels 4/8, bridge approaches, rotated/covered structures, overlapping transparency, pause/restart and phone audio are still required to close the corresponding issues."},{"title":"Development & release","url":"development.html","text":"Development, QA and release context [Documentation home](README.md) · [Architecture](ARCHITECTURE.md) · [Issue tracker](ISSUES.md) Local setup Use Node.js 22 or newer and the locked dependencies. Run commands from the repository root. sh npm ci npm run dev -- --host 0.0.0.0 Production checks and static build: npm test npm run build Vite writes dist/; npm run preview -- --host 0.0.0.0 serves that build when a browser-enabled environment is available. Local font assets are included. WebGL 2 is required by the current renderer. Audio requires browser user interaction; initial play or enabling sound initiates activation. Documentation maintenance Markdown in docs/ and the root README is the editable source. Generated HTML is checked in under docs/html/, including local CSS, navigation/search JavaScript and Mermaid. To refresh and validate: sh npm run docs:build npm run docs:check Open docs/html/index.html directly in a browser. The generator resolves links among the documented pages, creates heading anchors and a page table of contents, and emits an offline search index. Documentation checks also parse all Mermaid diagrams and exercise search, focus, copy cleanup and theme rerendering using jsdom; these DOM substitutes do not verify native browser rendering. Edit Markdown, not generated HTML. Regeneration must be deterministic. Keep third-party diagram code and license notices together. Adding a page requires adding it to the generator's explicit document list, then rebuilding and checking links. Test map Tests What they support ------ tests/simulation.test.js Herd delivery, body collision, reward/profile behavior tests/companions.test.js Largest connected herd, holds, attendance, barriers tests/world.test.js Generation, route/footprint validity, clearance and seeded fixtures tests/herding-journey.test.js Entire flocks delivered with ordinary simulated dog inputs in selected levels tests/progression.test.js Tier cost/unlock/activation and legacy data validation tests/ui.test.js Production controller against DOM/audio substitutes; navigation, rewards, restart and input tests/render.test.js Geometry, camera matrices, culling, batches and disposal tests/nature.test.js Wildlife, weather, resource budgets and cleanup tests/audio.test.js Audio activation races, scheduling, ambience and disposal sh Targeted examples; not a substitute for relevant integration checks. node --test tests/ui.test.js tests/audio.test.js node --test tests/world.test.js tests/herding-journey.test.js No screenshot or human listening claim follows from a mocked Canvas/WebGL/WebAudio test. Record real device/browser, revision, scenario and evidence for native touch, scrolling, visibility, audio and GPU performance. Large headless triangle counts are not phone FPS. CI and current publishing behavior The source branch is flock_3D_chatgpt-work. [The workflow](../.github/workflows/deploy-dev2.yml) runs npm ci, npm test and npm run build, then uploads dist/. Its artifact upload does not publish Pages. GitHub Pages uses the separate gh-pages branch and its native deployment workflow. Current mismatch: [the preparation script](../scripts/deploy-pages.mjs) still copies the build into flock/dev2/, giving https://novydhillon.github.io/Flock/flock/dev2/. F-025 is P0: migrate the output directory to dev2/, giving https://novydhillon.github.io/Flock/dev2/. That migration is not implemented by this documentation update. Do not treat the existing script as a compliant release procedure until corrected. Release checklist after F-025 is implemented 1. Read the latest issue/requirements decisions and fetch source and gh-pages. Require a clean intended source revision; do not overwrite another publisher's newer Pages head. 2. Pass relevant tests and production build. Stage only the intended dev2/ output and any explicitly agreed old-URL redirect. Preserve the root, existing flock/dev/ and unrelated content. Do not alter saved profile keys. 3. Review the Pages-tree diff against the latest parent. Verify relative asset paths and the /Flock/ repository URL prefix. Record the source SHA associated with the build. 4. Commit/push Pages with a checked expected parent/ref. Wait for successful native Pages deployment, not just successful source CI. 5. Fetch the new live HTML and every generated asset; compare bytes/hashes with the build. Run real-browser smoke checks when available. Record Pages SHA, workflow result, canonical URL and any validation limits. Documentation HTML is a repository deliverable in docs/html/; it is not automatically copied into the game build or published as another Pages site. A future hosted docs URL needs an explicit publishing location and preserved-content review. Agent handoff Use isolated worktrees for parallel owned files; integrate commits only after inspection. Blind reviewers should receive the candidate, task/requirements and scope, not historical scores or desired wording. Record confirmed problems, concrete evidence and checks. A threshold score is not a reason to relabel unverified browser behavior as tested. Before ending a session, update the issue execution record, regenerate HTML, run documentation checks, commit/push the intended branch and state exactly what was deployed. Do not close open features just because their requirements were documented."},{"title":"Issue tracker","url":"issues.html","text":"FLOCK — Remaining issues and change tracker Updated: 14 October 2026 (America/Vancouver). Repository: novydhillon/Flock. Working branch: flock_3D_chatgpt-work. Baseline source: 88ddb66495acaee94bfbc4653ccc818dfe459bc0 (Restart Game revision). Current live game (incorrect path): https://novydhillon.github.io/Flock/flock/dev2/. Required deployment URL: https://novydhillon.github.io/Flock/dev2/ (F-025, highest priority). This document records future work; creating it does not implement the changes below. How agents should use this document 1. Read this tracker, README and docs/QUALITY.md; inspect current code and branch state before starting. Public level numbers here are one-based; code indexes are zero-based. 2. Claim an issue by recording owner, branch/worktree, status and date. Use isolated worktrees for parallel implementation; coordinate ownership of shared files. Do not mark screenshot reports reproduced from a still image alone. 3. Confirm design decisions listed under each issue before coding dependent behavior. Explicit user requirements take precedence over proposed defaults. Preserve portrait, single-thumb play and the cheerful art direction. 4. Record reproduction level/seed, expected versus observed behavior, implementation commit, meaningful checks, reviewer findings and remaining limits. Use statuses: Open → In progress → Review → Done, or Blocked with the reason. 5. Run relevant regression checks and the production build. Use fresh blind reviewers for a substantial gameplay/rendering batch; give them requirements and the candidate, without earlier scores or implementation rationale. Fix confirmed defects and recheck. Do not claim visual or phone validation from headless tests. 6. A completed source change and a live release are separate milestones. Record deployment commit and verification separately. Resolve F-025 before the next game release: publish to dev2/ on the latest gh-pages tree, giving the public URL /Flock/dev2/. The old flock/dev2/ destination is superseded. Preserve unrelated content and handle the old URL explicitly as part of migration. Changes to this tracker alone do not require a game deployment. Priority: P0 = highest priority; complete before other queued work. P1 = correctness/gameplay foundation; P2 = planned feature/polish; P3 = broader validation/optimization. All open items are unassigned until explicitly claimed. Open work overview ID Priority Type Item Status Depends on ------------------ F-025 P0 Deployment Correct public deployment URL to /Flock/dev2/ Open —; first queued task F-001 P1 Reported defect Path-corner polygon flicker Open — F-002 P1 Reported defect Bark effect above ground and bridge deck Open — F-003 P2 Art/behavior Detailed squirrel; species-appropriate motion; no spinning Open — F-004 P2 Art Flowing-water texture Open — F-005 P1 Geometry/gameplay Perpendicular crossings; bridges 25% wider than paths Open — F-006 P2 Gameplay Gates requiring player opening from level 20 Open F-005 F-007 P2 Environment Stone walls on selected levels Open F-006 interaction contract F-008 P2 Art Soft textured clouds at 10–25% opacity Open — F-009 P1 Gameplay/economy Arrival countdown; partial-delivery scoring Open F-010 F-010 P1 Correctness Goal shape matches enclosure Open — F-011 P2 Feedback Blue dog-tail light; red bark flash Open F-002 shared bark event F-012 P2 Gameplay Level-gated wanderers and short bolting Open F-005, F-009 balance F-013 P2 Known review concern Level-5 pacing spike Open F-005; retest after F-009/F-012 F-014 P1 Validation gap Real-phone controls, appearance and sound Open Feature batches F-015 P3 Validation gap Mobile GPU performance and transition stability Open Rendering batches F-016 P1 Visibility Camera-facing barn/covered-structure cutaway Open F-010 shared enclosure geometry F-017 P2 Wildlife Detailed hopping rabbits; random size-scaled wildlife counts Open F-003, F-022 F-018 P1 QA tooling Dev-only level, currency and animal-placement menu Open — F-019 P2 Environment Add bushes Open — F-020 P2 Environment Increase tree size Open — F-021 P2 Art Richer bush/tree detail and textures Open F-019, F-020 F-022 P2 Wildlife/weather Species retreat as weather worsens Open F-017; shared weather state with F-023/F-024 F-023 P2 Weather/feedback Weather transitions, clouds, lighting, lightning and thunder Open F-008 F-024 P2 Weather/surfaces Accumulating wetness, puddles, splashes and river response Open F-004; shared weather state with F-023 Screenshot evidence Picture numbers refer to the user's attachment order, including the duplicate uploads. Picture Filename Evidence visible / reported --------- 1 IMG_8372.jpeg / IMG_8372(1).jpeg Level 4, 10/14 delivered; black triangular/striped artifacts at path corners. User reports flickering. 2 IMG_8367.jpeg / IMG_8367(1).jpeg Level 8; bark ring is visible around but hidden beneath bridge planks. 3 IMG_8368.jpeg / IMG_8368(1).jpeg Level 9; squirrel appears as a simple body and tail. User reports spinning in place. 4 IMG_8364.jpeg / IMG_8364(1).jpeg Level 8; black triangular artifacts at winding path bends near crossing. The images are visible in the conversation. Their advertised local attachment paths were unavailable during earlier reads; binaries are not embedded in this repository. Preserve these filenames and descriptions when handing off. Static images establish visible artifacts, not their temporal behavior or root cause. Acceptance criteria and implementation notes F-025 — Correct the deployment URL (P0 — highest priority) Requirement: deploy the standalone game at https://novydhillon.github.io/Flock/dev2/ instead of https://novydhillon.github.io/Flock/flock/dev2/. This issue takes precedence over all other open work. Accept when: the current standalone build loads directly at the required URL, including on a fresh visit/reload, with all JavaScript, styles, fonts and other assets resolving successfully beneath /Flock/dev2/. The gh-pages output directory is dev2/, not flock/dev2/; /Flock/ is GitHub Pages' repository URL prefix, not an extra output directory. Verify deployed file hashes against the intended production build and record the Pages commit/workflow result and final URL. Implementation scope: inspect and update scripts/deploy-pages.mjs, deployment/build configuration, README, tracker instructions and any other hard-coded publish destinations or game links. Keep relative asset support. Preserve the Pages root, existing flock/dev/ and all unrelated files. Do not clear or rename saved-profile keys as part of this URL correction; verify existing progress remains available at the new path on the same origin. Migration decision: explicitly record what happens to the previous /Flock/flock/dev2/ URL (for example, a redirect to the canonical URL). Do not leave two independently maintained game copies or delete unrelated legacy content. Any redirect must retain working navigation and use the correct repository prefix. Validation: inspect the proposed Pages-tree diff for the correct directory and preserved unrelated blobs; check the new live entry point and every referenced asset after successful publication. Update the tracker’s current-live URL only after verification. Documentation-only creation of this issue does not itself migrate the deployment. F-001 — Path-corner polygon flicker Requirement: eliminate polygon flickering at path corners (pictures 1 and 4). Accept when: sharp and gentle turns have continuous textured coverage without black triangles, overlapping flicker, holes or abrupt spikes during camera movement. Check default levels 4 and 8 and multiple seeds/directions on a real renderer. Investigate: src/render.js ribbon triangulation, tight-turn/self-overlap geometry, shoulders, depth offsets and shadow behavior. Degenerate triangles, overlap and z-fighting are hypotheses, not established causes. Add meaningful geometry checks; rendered verification is still required. F-002 — Bark effect above ground and bridge deck Requirement: bark remains visible above path, grass, ground and bridge planks; fences and railings still occlude it (picture 2). Accept when: bark works on land, center/end of each bridge and both approaches, with no deck clipping and normal occlusion by raised obstacles. Do not force the ring over every object by disabling depth testing globally. Code evidence: MeadowRenderer.bark() in src/render.js copies the dog's position, then assigns a fixed .08 world height. Inspect surface-following height over the expanding ring, not only its origin. Pause/cleanup must retain bounded effect resources. F-003 — Squirrel detail and movement Requirement: recognizable, more detailed squirrel; it must not spin in place, and must move like a squirrel (expanded user report, 14 October 2026). Accept when: distinct head/ears, face, legs/paws and bushy tail remain readable at game scale; heading follows actual movement, with stable idle/blocked poses and believable short turns. Wildlife still avoids Pip, the robotic flock and helpers and obeys ground collision. Use recognizable squirrel scurrying/bounding, brief alert pauses and coordinated legs/body/tail motion, rather than a sliding body or continuous circular orbit. Observe idle, blocked, turning and fleeing squirrels, not only open-field movement. Real-renderer observation is needed to judge motion quality. Files: src/nature.js; reusable art may belong in src/models.js. Current heading follows desired direction even when occupancy blocks movement; investigate that distinction without treating it as a proved sole cause. F-004 — Flowing water Requirement: add water texture conveying running water. Accept when: subtle moving ripples/flow follow each river's local direction across bends; bridges remain clear, banks have no seams, and water continues beyond map bounds. Pause freezes animation. Reuse/dispose resources and assess mobile cost. Collision stays tied to the river geometry. Files: src/render.js, src/world.js, renderer tests. F-005 — Bridge and crossing alignment Requirement: bridges and path segments crossing water are perpendicular to the river at the crossing. Bridge width is 1.25 × crossing path width. Minimize unnecessary bridge extension into dry ground so animals do not get stuck. Accept when: orientation uses the local river tangent, not a global axis; approaching/exiting paths align with the deck; mesh, terrain height, deck occupancy and rails share the same footprint. Retain enough landing overlap to cross both banks safely. Validate cow-sized bodies, helpers, the full initial flock, alternative routes, all campaign levels and representative seeds. Files: src/world.js, src/render.js, src/models.js, world/journey tests. Preserve the solved level-2 retrieval case; narrower replacement geometry must not reintroduce its stall. Specify whether the 25% comparison excludes decorative shoulders before implementation. F-006 — Operable gates from level 20 Requirement: introduce gates that need to be opened on higher levels (20+). Accept when: closed gates visibly and physically block dog/flock/helpers; an accessible single-thumb action opens them; geometry, collision, navigation and minimap agree on state. Levels 1–19 retain their existing accessible openings. Generated routes remain solvable and restart resets gate state. Decision to resolve: proximity action, auto-open interaction or dedicated contextual button; whether gates close again. Do not add a second-thumb requirement. Current gate openings do not implement this requested closed/open state. F-007 — Stone wall variants Requirement: use stone walls instead of fences on some levels. Accept when: selected deterministic levels have readable stone boundaries, consistent visible/physical footprints and usable entrances. Define whether walls block herding signals and how gates join them; raised walls should occlude effects correctly. Keep goals and route clearance valid. Decision to resolve: level/theme distribution and wall height. Candidate files: src/world.js, src/models.js, src/render.js, collision tests. F-008 — Soft textured clouds Requirement: clouds may be overlapping blurred soft-edge blob textures instead of models, remaining semi-transparent at 10–25%. Accept when: soft blobs form moving puffy clouds without hard edges or obvious planes; they do not obscure play. Preserve drifting movement and soft ground shadows, pause behavior and resource cleanup. Validate overlapping transparency and mobile rendering. Working interpretation to confirm: material/composited opacity 0.10–0.25 (not 75–90% opacity). Avoid stacking blobs into a much darker or opaque cloud. Current cloud material opacity is .5; see src/nature.js. F-009 — Delivery countdown and score Requirement: the first flock animal entering the goal starts a 30–60 second countdown, chosen by level complexity. End the level when time reaches zero or the last animal arrives. Score reflects animals delivered and displays score / possible score. Accept when: a single first valid delivery starts one timer; no pre-arrival timer; duration stays within 30–60 seconds; all-delivered ends early; timeout produces a partial result. Final counts and score settle once, late arrivals cannot change the finalized result, pause freezes gameplay time, and replay/restart clears transient state. Display delivered count and earned/possible score consistently in play and results. Preserve player-attended delivery; helpers cannot complete the level alone. Decisions required: complexity-to-duration rule; score per animal/type; whether score and spendable bolts are identical; partial result's next-level unlock rule; replay/best-score/reward policy and save migration. Proposed equal points per animal is not an approved formula. Prevent repeat reward farming while allowing meaningful improvement attempts. Existing full-flock reward and completion tests will need intentional revision. Files: src/simulation.js, src/main.js, src/progression.js, UI/style and persistence tests. Depends on F-010 so timer starts only inside the correct goal. F-010 — Goal shape matches enclosure Requirement: goal areas follow their pen, barn or fenced-field enclosure shape. Accept when: highlight, valid delivery region, minimap and enclosure footprint agree for rotated destinations; animals outside the wall or near a circular corner cannot score. Entrance remains clear and animal body radius is handled consistently. Check all three destination types across seeds. Code evidence: src/render.js currently draws a circular goal; src/simulation.js uses radial delivery distance. Derive rendering and admission from shared enclosure geometry rather than separate approximations. F-011 — Dog-tail light feedback Requirement: dog's tail light is blue normally and flashes red on bark. Accept when: Pip's blue light visibly flashes red for the bark feedback interval and returns blue reliably. Repeated bark, cooldown, pause and level restart leave no stuck-red state. Decide whether Buddy dog uses the same convention when it has an actual bark event. Do not recolor every shared cyan robotic material. Files: src/models.js, src/render.js, src/main.js; use the same bark event as F-002. F-012 — Wandering and bolting animals Requirement: up to 15% of animals may have a 33% chance to move randomly away from the flock when not actively herded. This begins at level 8. Such an animal has up to 10% chance to bolt away for 1–3 seconds; bolting begins after level 16 (level 17 onward). Accept when: levels 1–7 have no new wandering; levels 1–16 have no bolting. Eligible cohort never exceeds 15% of the flock; document integer rounding. Probabilities are tied to defined behavior opportunities/time, never rolled every frame. No fleeing through rails, walls, river or map fences; saved animals never wander. Active herding suppresses this behavior, and brief bolts end within the specified range. Pause/restart reset behavior safely and seeded tests exercise gates, duration, collision and statistical bounds without flaky expectations. Decisions required: selection/rounding, probability evaluation interval, wander distance/speed/cooldown, what counts as active herding (Pip versus companions), and whether the 10% is conditional on a wander event. Do not silently interpret a chance as a per-second or per-frame rate. Balance together with F-009 to avoid unfair late losses. Files: src/simulation.js, src/world.js, companion/journey/simulation tests. F-013 — Known level-5 pacing concern The fresh gameplay critic's unchanged input controller completed default levels 1–5 in 18.2, 17.2, 28.7, 38.7 and 208.0 simulated seconds. Level 5's jump warrants human playtesting; it is not proof of an impossible level or a measured human time. Accept when: document human attempts, retrieval/chokepoint behavior and difficulty progression; adjust only where evidence supports it. Re-run the existing ordinary-input journey policies after crossing/countdown/wandering changes. Avoid solving pacing by weakening collision or allowing autonomous completion. F-014 — Real-phone verification gap Still open: portrait readability, thumb control feel, native Safari double-tap zoom prevention, modal focus/scrolling, actual music/bird/weather balance, and rendered visual defects above. Headless checks cannot close these. Accept when: record device/browser, tested source/deployment revision, scenarios, results and screenshot/video evidence. Include restart confirmation/cancellation/persistence. Existing user screenshots provide evidence of specific visual issues; they do not validate every input/audio scenario. F-015 — Performance verification gap Real phone GPU frame rate and level-transition memory stability remain unmeasured. The existing bounded submission/resource tests are useful but do not establish FPS. Production JavaScript is roughly 576 kB / 155 kB gzip, with an advisory Vite chunk-size warning; this is an optimization consideration, not a confirmed gameplay bug. Accept when: measure normal/large/split herds, later expansive worlds, weather/cloud effects and repeated transitions on representative phones. Record frame-time distribution, load behavior and resource growth. Set a device-specific performance target before judging success. F-016 — See Pip and the flock under cover Requirement: barns and other covered structures must not obscure nearby Pip or flock animals. When they walk under cover, the roof and wall nearest the camera become transparent so the player can see them. Accept when: camera-facing roof/wall sections fade while occluding Pip or any flock animal beneath the structure, including animals close to the camera, partially entering/exiting and split groups. Select sections from the current camera view, including rotated structures. Restore their normal appearance when no longer obstructing the actors, without flicker or rapid threshold toggles. Keep distant/non-obstructing structure parts readable. Transparency changes visibility only; collision, goal admission and enclosure boundaries remain intact. Files/checks: src/models.js, src/render.js, renderer tests. Keep roof/wall sections independently controllable; check transparent draw ordering against animals, bark, weather and clouds. Verify with actual rendered views, not only material flags. F-017 — Rabbits and variable wildlife populations Requirement: add rabbits alongside squirrels. Rabbits must be recognizably detailed and move like rabbits. Rabbit and squirrel counts are random and depend on level size. Accept when: ears, face, body, hind legs and tail distinguish rabbits at normal portrait zoom. Hopping has coordinated crouch, push-off, airborne and landing poses, with believable idle/turn/flee transitions; avoid sliding or spinning. Both species avoid Pip/flock/helpers and obey ground barriers. Population varies with usable level area and randomness, with bounded counts for performance and repeatable seeded fixtures for testing. Weather visibility is handled by F-022, not by permanently deleting a level's base population. Decisions to resolve: area-to-count ranges/caps, whether either species may be absent, and spawn spacing/habitat. Do not silently replace the requested randomness with a fixed count per level. Files: src/nature.js, src/models.js, wildlife/resource tests. Coordinate squirrel motion work with F-003. F-018 — Dev-build QA menu Requirement: add a QA menu available in development builds. Testers can jump to any level 0–100, add bolts, remove bolts, and pause the simulation while moving individual flock animals to reproduce scenarios such as animals stuck in game-hindering positions. Accept when: the menu is clearly marked as QA and available in the deployed development build as well as the intended local development configuration. Release builds expose no QA entry point or enabled mutation handlers. Testers can choose a level without campaign unlock restrictions, enter a bolt amount to add/remove, pause gameplay and select/reposition a particular animal. Currency remains finite and nonnegative. Selection and placement work with touch and mouse and identify the selected animal clearly. Editing does not accidentally steer Pip, resume the simulation, advance countdowns, award progress or trigger delivery. Resume uses the edited scenario normally. Decisions required: the campaign currently has 100 public levels (1–100), represented by indexes 0–99. Preserve the requested 0–100 selector requirement pending an explicit mapping for 0 and 100; do not silently clamp 100 to another level or create an unintended extra campaign level. Define whether test currency/progress is isolated from normal saves, whether placement permits invalid positions deliberately, and how the tester resets/exports a scenario (level, seed, animal ID, position). Prefer an explicit placement override if intentionally testing collision-invalid cases. Files: build configuration, src/main.js, src/style.css, src/simulation.js, controller/profile tests. QA pause must permit manual edits while freezing all normal simulation; gameplay pause alone is insufficient. Exercise development and release builds separately to verify gating. F-019 — Bushes Requirement: add bushes to the environment. Accept when: organic bush clusters vary in shape/placement and fit fields/woodland edges without hiding critical controls, destinations or crossings. Generation preserves safe spawns, flock staging space and route clearance. Explicitly decide whether bushes are decorative or physical; if physical, mesh and collision footprints must agree and all routes remain traversable. Include visibility/culling and resource cleanup checks. Files: src/world.js, src/models.js, src/render.js, scenery/world tests. Coordinate appearance with F-021 rather than duplicating the assets. F-020 — Larger trees Requirement: increase the size of trees. Accept when: trees are visibly larger at the normal camera scale and remain proportionate to Pip, animals and structures. Maintain readable play space and valid routes/entrances. If trunk or canopy dimensions change, update placement clearance, physical footprints where applicable, bounds and shadow/culling data together. Verify that larger near-camera trees do not introduce new actor-visibility problems; F-016 covers structures specifically. Decision to resolve: target scale or size range and variation across levels. src/models.js, src/world.js and src/render.js are likely touch points. F-021 — Bush and tree surface detail Requirement: add more detail and textures to bushes and trees. Accept when: foliage has readable volume, color/leaf variation and softened organic forms; trunks/branches have bark detail. Preserve the established cheerful handmade appearance and consistency between new bushes and larger trees. Avoid flat uniform blobs, repetitive obvious tiling and excessive fine-detail shimmer at portrait scale. Assess shader/texture memory, draw submissions, shadows and mobile performance; shared/cached resources must dispose correctly. Files: src/models.js, src/render.js, scenery/resource tests. Dependencies F-019/F-020 establish the base shapes and size targets. F-022 — Weather-aware wildlife and shelter Requirement: light rain and light wind still allow butterflies, birds, squirrels, rabbits and other wildlife. As weather worsens, smaller animals disappear/hide first; larger animals remain until more extreme conditions, then hide too. Accept when: light conditions do not indiscriminately suppress wildlife. A documented per-species severity threshold/order causes gradual retreat/hiding as weather intensifies, and plausible return after it eases. Avoid abrupt simultaneous despawns, visible animals frozen in place, repeated threshold flicker or frame-rate-dependent population changes. Hiding wildlife still avoids the flock; ground animals never flee through barriers. Define behavior for airborne species and currently supported weather types, including snow. Coordinate shelter/visibility state with population generation in F-017. Decisions required: species size categories, severity thresholds/hysteresis, shelter/exit behavior and return delay. The user supplied the ordering principle, not exact numeric thresholds. Files: src/nature.js, wildlife tests. Consume the same weather severity/transition state as F-023/F-024. F-023 — Worsening-weather atmosphere and storms Requirement: when weather changes toward rain or storms, increase cloud cover and change lighting to signal worsening conditions. If lightning occurs, show flashes and play thunder sounds. Accept when: transitions adjust cloud coverage and ground lighting smoothly, preserving legibility of Pip, flock and goal. Cloud coverage works with F-008's soft semi-transparent cloud requirement; resolve any storm opacity exception explicitly rather than silently overriding it. Lightning flashes and thunder belong to the same bounded event, with sensible audio timing, and do not create uncontrolled flash bursts or stacked audio sources. Mute, pause, backgrounding, replay and level transitions handle storm effects and pending sounds consistently; clean up event resources. Record real-phone visual/listening checks. Decisions required: severity model/transition timing, whether weather changes during a level, lightning frequency/intensity and thunder timing. A new within-level weather system must explicitly define pause/restart/seed behavior; current fixed per-level choices must not be assumed to provide it. Files: src/nature.js, src/render.js, src/audio.js, weather/audio/resource tests. Provide shared rain/wind severity and event state to F-022 and F-024. F-024 — Rain accumulation, puddles and water response Requirement: ground appearance reflects rainfall. Light rain has little effect; sustained heavier rain slowly darkens grass green and dirt tracks brown. Rainstorms produce standing puddles and splashes when animals walk through them. River water also reflects the weather change. Accept when: wetness accumulates with rain amount over simulation time, rather than switching instantly to a fixed dark material. Grass and paths respond distinctly while remaining readable. Storm puddles occupy plausible ground locations without clipping across bridges or raised surfaces, and flock movement through them generates brief localized splashes; stationary animals do not emit continuous splash bursts. River surface appearance responds coherently to rain/storm intensity while retaining directional flow (F-004). These visual effects do not silently alter movement/collision. Pause freezes accumulation and events; restart/level replacement clears transient resources correctly. Preserve mobile performance under large moving flocks. Decisions required: accumulation/saturation and drying rates, puddle formation thresholds/placement, river changes (for example ripples, roughness or color), and whether Pip/helpers/wildlife also splash. Those examples are implementation options, not approved extra mechanics. Files: src/nature.js, src/render.js, src/world.js, weather/renderer/resource tests. Use shared weather state from F-023 and check material sharing does not leak wetness into the next level. Previously repaired — retain as regressions These are not open defects unless reproduced again: drone selecting the largest herd and holding when Pip leaves; no unattended goal completion; unlocks (drone 10, Buddy 20); path variety/map extent/continuous rivers; scenery hill collision alignment; pending audio activation overriding pause; overdue music scheduling burst; the default level-2 bridge stall; full-game restart persistence and delayed-audio focus. See docs/QUALITY.md and current tests for scope and limitations. The latest Restart Game source passed CI tests/build and deployment byte verification. Historical blind scores 9.1 technical / 8.6 gameplay describe the earlier bounded review, not a review of these newly requested changes. Do not update scores without a new independent assessment. Execution record Copy one row per claimed issue; retain older entries when reopening work. Issue Owner Status Branch/worktree Source commit Checks and review evidence Deployment commit Date / next action ------------------------ — Unassigned Open — — — — Claim an issue before implementation Decisions and session handoff Record approved answers to the decisions above here, with date and source. At session end list changed issue IDs, remaining blockers, commands/results, branch heads and the next reproducible action. A blocked design decision does not prevent independent visual repairs. Suggested order: F-025 first (P0 deployment correction) → F-001/F-002/F-010 → F-005 → F-009 and gate/wall contracts → F-006/F-007/F-012. Art items F-003/F-004/F-008/F-011 can proceed in coordinated worktrees; F-013–F-015 remain cross-cutting validation work. Update recorded 14 October 2026: expanded F-003 squirrel locomotion and added F-016–F-024 for visibility, rabbits/population, dev QA tooling, vegetation and linked weather/wildlife/surface behavior. All remain Open and unassigned. Coordinate F-022/F-023/F-024 around one weather-state contract; F-018 can support reproductions without becoming a runtime dependency of other fixes. Update recorded 14 October 2026: F-025 added as P0/highest priority. Required canonical URL is https://novydhillon.github.io/Flock/dev2/; the existing nested deployment remains the current live location until migration is implemented and verified."},{"title":"Quality record","url":"quality.html","text":"Flock dev2 revision quality record The latest fresh blind-review retry scored 9.1/10 for technical implementation and 8.6/10 for gameplay implementation after fixes. Neither critic found a remaining confirmed blocking defect in the bounded recheck. The technical review reaches 9/10; the overall game experience remains unverified at that score. Actual phone appearance, sound balance, Safari gestures, touch scrolling, human campaign pacing and GPU performance remain open validation tasks. Review packets excluded README, quality records, writer history and requested score thresholds. Fresh critics independently inspected source and exercised simulation, geometry and controller behavior. An earlier diagnostic gameplay review accidentally read historical scores; it is excluded from the blind final assessment. Review Before fixes Final Confirmed issues addressed ------:---:--- Runtime diagnostic 8.0 — Unattended goal admission, distant rendering workload Fresh source/technical critic 8.4 8.9 Cohesion through fences/trees; unnecessary engine exports; singleton companion hold Fresh gameplay critic 8.4 8.7 Drone treating an isolated singleton as a main herd Fresh technical retry 8.8 9.1 Pending audio activation undoing pause; unbounded overdue music scheduling Fresh gameplay retry 8.2 8.6 Default level-two bridge retrieval stall under the test controller The retry used two new critics with no earlier scores or writer context. The default second meadow initially left all eight sheep unsaved after 400 simulated seconds under the existing test controller. Widening only its first bridge from 8.5 m to 11.5 m lets the same unchanged controller deliver all eight in 17.2 seconds. Later early crossings remain 8.5 m, and the map, river and solid rails are retained. Audio activation now uses generation checks so a later pause/disposal/request invalidates an older pending browser resume. Controller startup and sound enabling also respect the current game mode. Delayed music callbacks skip missed beats and cap work; the independent 60-second stall probe schedules one future musical step instead of 212 overdue steps. The gameplay critic independently completed default levels 1–5 with the unchanged legitimate-input controller: 18.2, 17.2, 28.7, 38.7 and 208.0 simulated seconds. The larger jump at level five is a pacing concern for human playtesting, not proof of an impossible level or a measured human completion time. Earlier review findings also drove repairs to dry bridge rail collision, rare seeded spawn clearance, companion clear-ground attendance, workshop focus restoration and wildlife avoidance of delivered animals/helpers. Ground buddies now detour around bridge ends. Cohesion and alignment respect physical barriers. Pip can call over low wood fences/bridge rails, while bodies, companion membership and goal admission use strict ground paths; barns, water and trees still shield Pip's call. Verified checks - 93 automated tests passed at the blind-review retry snapshot. They exercise actual simulation/profile logic, production controller code, real Three.js geometry/matrices, resource ownership and synthesized audio lifecycle. - All routes across 100 default levels are physically traversed by cow-sized bodies. Independent sweeps found no blocked routes or unsafe spawns in 500 additional generated worlds. A separate 1,600-fixture spawn sweep verified the rare-seed repair. - Full original flocks in levels 2, 3, 6, 10 and 16 are delivered across four directions by a bounded simulated player using ordinary dog inputs, safe bridge/pen circling and actual collision. No animal positions or saved flags are changed by that policy. This does not establish full-flock completion of every campaign level, route, seed or player strategy. - Retry critics independently checked 800 and 3,000 generated worlds before the first-bridge widening, with no unsafe actual-radius spawns or cow-clearance route failures. The refreshed default campaign and first-bridge geometry regressions also pass after widening. - Independent integration of actual controller and FarmAudio, with browser surfaces substituted, confirms menu/visibility pause during pending activation settles paused, with zero music schedulers and a ducked music gain. Ten audio lifecycle tests cover both completion orders, mute, disposal and delayed scheduling. - Regression checks cover main-herd selection, isolated-straggler retrieval, fifteen-minute stationary holds, no unattended admission, clear attendance through barriers, singleton standby and reactivation, and exact level-10/20 companion gates with preserved legacy ownership. - Renderer checks establish finite camera/pose matrices, conservative culling, visible shadow retention, exact articulated pose restoration, bounded scenery and deterministic disposal. Level-100 actor submissions are 238,604 triangles with the full flock visible, 80,712 in the tested split view and zero at a distant view. These are geometry/submission counts, not measured FPS or rendered pixels. - Explicit geometry constructor references reduce production JavaScript from about 786 kB/212 kB gzip to 575 kB/154 kB gzip. The remaining Vite chunk-size warning is advisory; no runtime/font asset CDN is required. Verification boundary DOM, Canvas, WebGL and WebAudio substitutes support headless checks. No approved browser/phone environment was available for rendered screenshots, real touchscreen interactions, listening or GPU profiling. The tests therefore do not prove visual polish, native Safari zoom/scroll behavior, perceived audio quality or mobile frame rate. Device priorities are portrait readability and control feel, double-tap bark without page zoom, workshop scrolling, large-flock rendering/level-transition performance, and bird/weather balance through phone speakers. These checks remain necessary before claiming a verified 9/10 game experience. Documentation maintenance note — 14 October 2026 The later Restart Game revision added six controller regressions, bringing the source suite to 99 tests; its source CI passed. The historical review scores above were not rerun for that small feature. See [requirements](REQUIREMENTS.md), [system context](ARCHITECTURE.md) and [issue tracker](ISSUES.md) for implemented versus queued behavior. Documentation content/HTML reviews are recorded separately in docs/REVIEW.md; their scores do not rate the game itself."},{"title":"Documentation reviews","url":"review.html","text":"Documentation review record Scope: requirements, system context and the generated HTML documentation. These scores rate documentation, not the game. Updated 14 October 2026. Blind review method Two fresh agents received the same committed candidate (bd227d3) in separate detached worktrees. They were instructed not to inspect Git history, sibling workspaces, other agents' findings or earlier documentation scores. They received the user’s documentation task without a target score. One reviewed content against source/tests; the other reviewed HTML fidelity, navigation, accessibility and behavior with DOM substitutes. Historical game scores in QUALITY.md were explicitly excluded as documentation evidence. Review First pass Confirmed findings Follow-up ------:------ Content 8.8/10 Root README still gave superseded publish instructions; helper validation/scope claims overstated; promised review record absent Resolved; follow-up 9.2/10 at 7899759 HTML format/content 8.6/10 Appearance toggle left diagram theme unchanged; failed legacy clipboard copy leaked a textarea Resolved; follow-up 9.0/10 at 7899759 The HTML critic’s first-pass categories were content/fidelity 9.1, format/navigation 8.5, accessibility 8.2 and functionality 8.6. Both first passes exceeded the user’s 8/10 threshold; confirmed issues were still repaired rather than ignored. Final follow-up results The original critics independently rechecked 7899759: content 9.2/10, HTML overall 9.0/10 (content/fidelity 9.1, format/navigation 8.7, accessibility 8.7, functionality 9.2). Both reported no remaining confirmed defect or blocker. This is follow-up validation after a blind first pass, not a new blind review. The final review-record edit only records these results. Both reran npm run docs:check: 11 pages, 614 local links/anchors, nine parsed diagrams and four passing DOM behavior tests. The HTML reviewer additionally held a diagram render open across rapid theme changes and confirmed no overlap with the correct final theme; repeated false clipboard returns, thrown exceptions and async Clipboard API rejection left no temporary textareas. Native browser limits below still apply. Evidence - Independent content review ran all 99 source tests, the documentation checks and all three runnable architecture/gameplay/world examples successfully. - Both critics independently ran the first candidate’s deterministic generator/link checks: 10 pages, 551 local links/anchors and nine diagrams. - The HTML critic parsed all nine diagram sources with the actual bundled Mermaid library; DOM substitutes exercised search, keyboard focus, clipboard and appearance persistence and reproduced both UI issues. - Revisions clarify that F-025 remains P0 and unimplemented; the preparation script checks only dist/index.html, replaces the old nested folder and also writes/stages .nojekyll at the Pages root. - HTML revisions serialize diagram theme rerenders and clean up temporary clipboard fields in a finally block. The source alternatives beneath diagrams remain readable. - The generator checks local document/source links and anchors, current generated output, offline runtime assets and traceability of all F-001–F-025 requirements. CI runs documentation checks before source tests/build. Verification limits No approved browser environment was available for real rendered screenshots, SVG layout, native dialogs, touchscreen interaction, actual file:// clipboard permission or print output. Source, parsed diagram syntax and DOM substitutes support the scores; they do not prove pixel-level visual quality. Manual browser smoke tests remain useful. The external 12ui design workflow was blocked by automatic approval review because sending the project brief to that service was not authorized. The documentation interface was built locally; no approval to disclose that brief is implied by these review scores. Maintenance Update this record when subsequent documentation changes receive new review evidence. Keep writer context and past documentation scores out of any new blind review packet. Rechecks by the original critics are follow-up validation, not a new blind first pass. Regenerate HTML after editing this record and keep requirements/issue statuses unchanged unless implementation evidence justifies a change."},{"title":"Project README","url":"project.html","text":"Flock: Little Robot, Big Pastures A cheerful 3D herding game for portrait phones. Guide a robotic sheepdog through handmade-looking countryside and bring your robotic flock home. Start with a small sheep flock, then tackle longer routes, larger groups, bridges, gates, and other destinations. Earn points for upgrades and autonomous helpers. Development branch: flock_3D_chatgpt-work. Documentation: [Start here](docs/README.md) · [HTML field guide](docs/html/index.html) · [Requirements](docs/REQUIREMENTS.md) · [Issue tracker](docs/ISSUES.md). Play the standalone development build at [novydhillon.github.io/Flock/flock/dev2/](https://novydhillon.github.io/Flock/flock/dev2/). The existing flock/dev/ game is preserved separately. Engine choice This build uses Three.js, with Vite for packaging. The requested ThreeRust engine at [threerust.com](https://threerust.com/) was investigated first. Its public offering is an early-access Rust/WebAssembly/WebGPU browser editor with RHAI scripting; a reusable runtime SDK suitable for this repository was not available through its public documentation. The user approved the Three.js fallback. This project does not claim to use ThreeRust or the separate three-rs/threers Rust projects. Three.js renders the world in true 3D with WebGL. The original characters and scenery use rounded clay-like shapes, colorful metal panels, visible mechanical joints, and glowing robot details. Run locally Use Node.js 22 or newer: sh npm ci npm run dev -- --host 0.0.0.0 Open the local URL printed by Vite. To run the simulation tests and build a standalone site: sh npm test npm run build npm run preview -- --host 0.0.0.0 The production build is written to dist/. Relative asset paths allow it to run beneath /Flock/flock/dev2/ without a backend or CDN dependency. Playing - Use one thumb on the movement control to steer the robotic dog. - Drag anywhere on the meadow to steer, or use WASD/arrow keys on desktop. Double-tap the meadow or tap Woof for a short herding burst. - Approach the flock from behind to guide it toward the highlighted destination. - Keep the flock together while following routes through the countryside. - Complete a pasture to earn points, then spend them on skills and helpers. - Six skills have three tiers each: Turbo paws, Friendly signal, Scout drone, Buddy dog, Happy bark, and Flock harmony. The workshop shows the next tier's effect, cost, and level requirement. Skills appear throughout the first fifteen levels; the drone starts at level 10, and the more expensive Buddy dog starts at level 20. - Companions attend the largest connected herd. When Pip leaves, they hold that herd in place. Recover isolated stragglers yourself and return to lead the delivery. - Music starts after a player interaction, following mobile browser audio rules. Use the sound control to mute it. The opening pasture teaches herding with a small sheep flock and a nearby pen. Later procedural pastures introduce longer routes, rivers and bridges, roads and gates, more animals, and new destinations. Progress is stored locally in the browser; clearing site data clears that progress. Deployment P0 correction pending (F-025): the required canonical URL is https://novydhillon.github.io/Flock/dev2/, with output under dev2/ on gh-pages. The current nested deployment and helper described below are superseded for the next release. Correct the helper and verify migration before publishing another game revision. See [development and release context](docs/DEVELOPMENT.md) and [F-025](docs/ISSUES.mdf-025--correct-the-deployment-url-p0--highest-priority). GitHub Pages currently publishes the repository's gh-pages branch. The game is deployed to flock/dev2/ within that branch, so its full URL includes the case-sensitive repository prefix /Flock/. .github/workflows/deploy-dev2.yml tests and builds pushes to flock_3D_chatgpt-work, then uploads the standalone dist/ artifact. These checks do not enter the publishing environment. The existing publication procedure replaced flock/dev2/ in the latest gh-pages tree with that build and committed/pushed the Pages branch. F-025 changes that destination to dev2/ before the next release. Preserve flock/dev/, the site root, and all unrelated files. GitHub Pages publishes the existing gh-pages branch through its native workflow. The following command demonstrates the existing helper’s behavior for inspection only; it still stages the wrong nested path until F-025 is implemented: sh npm run build node scripts/deploy-pages.mjs /tmp/flock-pages-review git -C /tmp/flock-pages-review diff --cached --stat The preparation script stages without committing or pushing, refuses an existing worktree destination, and checks only that dist/index.html exists. It replaces flock/dev2/ and also writes/stages the Pages-root .nojekyll; it does not validate every asset or guarantee that unrelated root content is unchanged. Inspect the entire staged diff. Its current behavior is not a compliant implementation of F-025. Scope This is a playable development build. It stores progress on-device and uses procedural geometry and generated material textures. It has no account system, online multiplayer, or cloud save service. The campaign contains 100 deterministic procedural meadows. Flock size increases to a maximum of 65; journey distance continues increasing afterward. Levels 1–5 have compact, single-route lessons. From level 6 the fenced roaming area expands, with one or two trails through level 15 and up to three afterward. Destinations rotate across compass directions, and winding trails connect real bridges and gate openings with clear staging space before the goal. Rivers continue beyond the perimeter; scenery hills stay outside playable ground. Sheep and cows use articulated, instanced leg poses at a stop-motion cadence. Grass and dirt have deterministic tileable color and bump textures, with decorative flowers, grasses and reeds. Puffy clouds drift with soft ground shadows. Later meadows cycle through sunshine, wind, light rain, and snow after level 8. Butterflies, birds and squirrels flee nearby robots; quiet bird and weather ambience shares the sound control. Automated checks cover simulation, upgrades, controller navigation, touch cancellation, denied storage, renderer matrices and resource disposal. The renderer tests use real Three.js geometry with a substituted GPU interface; they do not verify rendered pixels or phone frame rates. Actual mobile play, sound balance and GPU performance need device testing."}];
